// Made from the five Time documents in docs/systems by scripts/pilot-docs.mjs. Do not edit by hand.
// A test fails when this text and those files differ. Run npm run pilot:docs after a change to one of them.
export type TimeDoc = { key: string; title: string; path: string; text: string };
export const TIME_DOCS: TimeDoc[] = [{"key":"time","title":"Folder 01 Time","path":"docs/systems/TIME.md","text":"# Folder 01: Time\n\nDepartment I, Agency Assessment. Internal specification. Version 4.1, October 11, 2026. What the folder is, what goes on file, and how the agent runs it.\n\n## 1. About this document\n\n1.1 This is one of five documents for this folder. It states what the folder is. It is read by the person who builds the folder and the person who teaches it. It is edited in place when a decision changes.\n\n1.2 The authoritative copy is `docs/systems/TIME.md` in the repository. A Word file of this document is an export of that copy.\n\n1.3 The other four. **Folder 01 Time Operations** holds the fields, states, views, queries, and action permissions. It is at `docs/systems/TIME_OPERATIONS.md`. **Folder 01 Time Developments** holds the dated log of changes, errors, open issues, and ideas held for later. It is at `docs/systems/TIME_DEVELOPMENTS.md`. **Folder 01 Time Curriculum** holds the twelve questions, their options, and the teaching layer. It is at `docs/systems/TIME_CURRICULUM.md`. **Folder 01 Time Research** holds the evidence and the reasoning, as a running list. It is at `docs/systems/TIME_RESEARCH.md`.\n\n1.4 The revised Curriculum Version 2 and the updated Research were located in Google Drive on October 11, 2026. Both address the Version 4 direction. Both were copied into this repository on October 11, 2026, and the repository copy governs. See Developments 2.22. Their references and remaining reconciliation are in items 15.1 and 15.2. Their existing client copy and evidence are not changed by this specification revision.\n\n1.5 This document is not loaded into the agent. The agent runs on the Language Standard and on operating instructions built from this document.\n\n1.6 Client facing copy appears here as a marked block, in the exact words the client sees. Those blocks exist nowhere else.\n\n1.7 Version 4 is dated October 11, 2026. It revises Version 3 of August 28, 2026. Version 3 defined Time as building a schedule from conversation and measured how much of a client's time was accounted for. Version 4 defines Time as planning, execution, review, and follow through. The schedule stays. It is the system the folder runs on.\n\n1.8 Version 4.1, October 11, 2026, clarifies the resident-facing calendar that the schedule must provide. It does not change the definition of Time, the Enrollment-to-Orientation-to-Time order, the authority for File changes, or the conditions for a commitment.\n\n## 2. What it is\n\n2.1 **Name.** Time.\n\n2.2 **Descriptor.** Planning your commitments and following through on each one.\n\n2.3 **Definition.** Time covers how a client plans commitments, executes them, reviews what happened, and follows through on the work that remains. The agent establishes what has to happen, places it in the schedule within the client's circumstances, checks what happened, and keeps the remaining work on the schedule until its requirements are met. Time measures the client's ability to plan, execute, and review commitments under the conditions the client has set. Time does not measure how much a client does.\n\n2.4 The folder delivers five results, in this order.\n\n2.4.1 A schedule the client did not have to build. The agent assembles it from what the client says in conversation. A client can answer what time they got up. A client is never asked to fill an empty grid. The resident can view the calendar as intake begins and see established commitments take their places as the schedule is assembled.\n\n2.4.2 A Plan for each day. The Plan lists the commitments the client intends to execute that day.\n\n2.4.3 Follow through that the agent carries out. After a commitment is executed, the agent does the administrative work the client has authorized, schedules the work that remains, and keeps each open follow up active.\n\n2.4.4 A history of what happened to each commitment. The agent and the client use it to make the next Plan one the client can execute.\n\n2.4.5 Two figures from the schedule. The hours a week the client controls, and the longest unbroken block in a normal week.\n\n2.5 The client's ability to plan and execute is assessed twice. The first assessment is the Start and Target the client selects at Enrollment. The second is the outcomes that go on file during residency.\n\n2.6 Each outcome is compared with the conditions that client stated. Time holds no punctuality rule that applies to every client.\n\n2.7 A commitment to rest counts the same as every other commitment. A Plan is never graded by how many commitments it holds.\n\n2.8 Time works with two questions. Did today go as planned is the question of Daily Close. How many hours a week are mine, and where do they sit is the question the schedule answers. The folder list keeps the second one as the question for Time.\n\n2.9 Conversation is how the schedule gets built from nothing. The client can also edit the schedule by hand at any point. A client who adds and moves blocks by hand is never routed through the agent to do it.\n\n2.9.1 The schedule is visible as a calendar in day, week, and month views. The day and week views place known timed blocks at their actual times. The month view places dated entries on their dates. These views read and change the same maintained schedule; they are not separate calendars or copies of commitments.\n\n2.9.2 A calendar may display incomplete information without treating it as complete. A dated commitment whose start or end is not established remains identifiable, with its missing time visible; the software does not invent a start, end, or duration to fill a slot. A proposed or tentative time remains distinct from confirmed time under the approval rules.\n\n2.10 A block typed in by hand and a block built from a client's statement are the same once filed.\n\n### 2.11 Client copy, folder description\n\n> Time\n>\n> You do not have to build a schedule. You tell your agent what you need to do, and your agent builds the schedule from what you say.\n>\n> You and your agent make a plan for each day. Your agent asks what happened, updates your information where you have given permission, and puts the work that is left on your schedule.\n>\n> Your schedule shows how many hours a week are yours and where they sit.\n>\n> Did today go as planned?\n\n## 3. What goes on file\n\n3.1 Four kinds of entry go on file in this folder: a block, a commitment, a follow up, and ongoing work.\n\n### 3.2 The block\n\n3.2.1 A **block** is a period of time with a start, an end, and a label. Each block also carries the four fields below.\n\n3.2.2 **Observed or estimated.** Observed means it happened and the client knows it. Estimated means it is a guess, a usual amount, or a period that has not yet taken place.\n\n3.2.3 **Paid, unpaid, or neither.** Paid means the client is compensated for those hours. Unpaid means the time is required by the work and is not paid for. Travel to a shift is unpaid time. Neither means the block is unrelated to work.\n\n3.2.4 **Cost of.** Which commitment the block exists because of, when the block is not a commitment itself. Travel, preparation, and recovery are costs of the commitment they surround.\n\n3.2.5 **Planned or done.**\n\n### 3.3 The commitment\n\n3.3.1 A **commitment** is a specific action the client intends to execute. **Execution** is the carrying out of a commitment. Each commitment has a label and the block it is planned in.\n\n3.3.2 A commitment carries five conditions. The agent fills a condition only where it is relevant to that commitment. A condition the client has not stated stays unknown.\n\n| Condition | What goes on file |\n| :-- | :-- |\n| Timing | The required date and time, the deadline, the duration, and how far the timing can move |\n| Completion | What the client says must happen for the commitment to be fulfilled |\n| Consequences | What the client says a delay, a change, or a miss would affect. Separately, what the client later reports did happen |\n| Standards | Which minimums from the client's Standards folder apply |\n| Follow through | Each action required after the commitment is executed, with its owner, its timing, and the ongoing work it belongs to |\n\n3.3.3 The five conditions are internal requirements. They are not a script of five questions. A client can state more than one condition in one sentence.\n\n3.3.4 A commitment can go on a Plan when three facts are on file: the action, the client's authorization or an agreement already on file, and a date or time that is enough to schedule it. Each other condition is collected when it is relevant.\n\n3.3.5 **Outcome.** What happened to a commitment is held in four separate fields. No single label carries more than one of them.\n\n3.3.6 **Execution.** One of four values: planned, executed, partly executed, or did not happen. Execution is measured against the Completion condition the client stated.\n\n3.3.7 **Timing.** One of three values: as planned, changed, or not established. Timing is filed as changed when the actual time differs from the Plan. Where the client reported the time, the actual time is on file.\n\n3.3.8 **Consequences.** One of three values: reported, none confirmed, or not established. Reported means the client said what was affected, and each consequence is on file as its own entry. None confirmed means the client said no commitment or condition was affected. Not established means no information about consequences is on file.\n\n3.3.9 Not established is never read as none confirmed. The absence of a report does not prove that no harm happened.\n\n3.3.10 **Alternative.** Whether the client used an alternative that was agreed before the commitment was due, and which one.\n\n3.3.11 A changed time says nothing about execution. A commitment can be executed with changed timing and no consequence.\n\n3.3.12 Executing an action and finishing the larger responsibility are two separate facts. The outcome of a commitment is held apart from the status of its follow ups and of the ongoing work it belongs to. A commitment stays executed while linked work is open.\n\n### 3.4 The follow up\n\n3.4.1 A **follow up** is information, a confirmation, a client action, or an agent action that is still required after a conversation or a commitment.\n\n3.4.2 Each follow up carries four fields: its owner, its next action, the conversation or commitment it came from, and a status of open or closed. The owner is the client or the agent.\n\n### 3.5 Ongoing work\n\n3.5.1 **Ongoing work** is a continuing responsibility that contains more than one commitment or follow up. It can recur.\n\n3.5.2 Ongoing work carries a label, the recurrence the client has stated, and a link to each commitment and follow up inside it.\n\n3.5.3 One case. Laundry is ongoing work. Washing, drying, and putting clothes away are three commitments inside it. Washing can be executed while putting clothes away is still planned. The laundry stays active and Time schedules the remaining commitment.\n\n### 3.6 Rules governing the record\n\n3.6.1 A period in the future is estimated. No planned block is ever filed as observed.\n\n3.6.2 An estimate becomes observed only when the client says what happened. The agent does not promote it and does not round a figure into a fact.\n\n3.6.3 The hours a client controls are calculated from observed time only. Estimates stay on file and are excluded from the figure.\n\n3.6.4 No entry is invented. A statement the client did not make is not on file. Where a statement is unclear the agent asks.\n\n3.6.5 An expected consequence is never filed as a consequence that happened. The two are separate fields.\n\n3.6.6 A commitment is marked executed only when the client reports it or evidence on file proves it. A planned block does not prove that the commitment happened.\n\n3.6.7 Planned, estimated, and observed entries stay distinguishable in every view.\n\n3.6.8 Every entry keeps who wrote it: the client, the agent, or software.\n\n## 4. Routing\n\n4.1 This folder holds hours, commitments, and the work that follows a commitment. It does not hold the subject the hours were spent on. Where both are relevant, the hours and the commitment are filed here and the subject is filed in the folder named below.\n\n| What arrives | Filed in Time | Also filed in |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n| A shift | The hours worked | Career Development, Schedule, as the shift entry |\n| Travel to a shift | The hours, as a cost of that shift | Nothing. Travel is not its own commitment |\n| A vehicle, fare, or breakdown | Nothing | Housing Stability, Transportation |\n| Sleep | The hours | Nothing |\n| Insomnia or a sleep problem | The hours only | Agency Assessment, Health |\n| A medical appointment | The hours, with travel attached | Agency Assessment, Health, as the care |\n| A court or government appointment | The hours, with travel and waiting | Legal or Administration, as the matter |\n| Caring for a person | The hours and the relationship | Agency Assessment, Background, as the person |\n| Housework | The hours | Sanitation or Organization, as the task |\n| Seeing friends | The hours | Life Management, Socializing, as the relationship |\n| An evening spent on screens | The hours | Agency Assessment, Regulation, as what it did or did not restore |\n| Work on the client's own project | The hours | The relevant Career or Education folder |\n| Pay for hours worked | Nothing | Career Development, Salary |\n\n4.2 A block filed here with no hours attached is non compliant. If the client reported a subject and no time, it belongs in the other folder alone.\n\n4.3 A commitment with no time set stays on file with Timing unknown. It receives a block when the timing is established.\n\n### 4.4 Coordination across folders\n\n4.4.1 Work that starts in another folder is scheduled here. When a folder produces an action with a time, a deadline, or a recurrence, the action is placed in the schedule as a commitment linked to that folder.\n\n4.4.2 An executed commitment can require a change in another folder. The agent prepares each change in the folder it belongs to. Time keeps the link between the commitment and the change.\n\n4.4.3 Career Development, Schedule. The work schedule supplies planned shifts. Time holds what was worked.\n\n4.4.4 Life Management, Standards. Standards holds the client's minimums. Time holds which minimums apply to a commitment. Time never sets a minimum.\n\n4.4.5 Housing Stability, Inventory. Where the items from an executed purchase are known and Inventory maintains them, the Inventory changes are prepared in Inventory.\n\n4.4.6 Agency Assessment, Finance. A purchase amount from an executed commitment is filed in Finance. Salary holds no second copy of it.\n\n4.4.7 Career Development, Integrity. Integrity covers every commitment the client made, at work and outside work. Integrity reads the commitments already on file in Time. Integrity holds no second schedule and no second copy of a commitment.\n\n4.4.8 Time holds when a commitment is scheduled and what happened to it. Integrity holds the client's record of whether they did what they said they would do.\n\n## 5. How it works\n\n5.1 The agent runs the folder. The agent learns the client's circumstances through ordinary conversation. The agent establishes what has to happen, places it in the schedule, checks what happened, follows up, and keeps the remaining work active. The client reports their life, makes the decisions that belong to them, and carries out the actions only the client can do.\n\n5.2 **Agent and software.** The agent is the person who represents the client and answers for the work. Software carries out defined work under rules and permissions, on the agent's behalf. Software is never called the agent and never presents as a person. Language Standard 3.11 and 10.8.1.\n\n### 5.3 Planning\n\n5.3.1 A **Plan** is the list of commitments the client intends to execute on one day. The agent presents the commitments already known for that day and asks what is different from usual. The client confirms the Plan.\n\n5.3.2 The **schedule** is the recurring structure of the client's week. The agent maintains it from repeated Plans, outcomes, and client statements. A stated schedule does not override repeated outcomes that show the week runs differently.\n\n5.3.3 Before the first Plan is saved, the agent asks how realistic it feels to the client. A Plan the client calls unlikely is simplified or moved before it is saved.\n\n5.3.4 Time with Universe City is itself placed on the Plan. The first Plan asks what time Daily Close belongs in the client's day.\n\n### 5.4 Contact\n\n5.4.1 Contact has three origins.\n\n| Origin | What starts it |\n| :-- | :-- |\n| Client requested | The client asks for contact or for an action |\n| Permitted automatic | A standing permission already covers the action. Software carries it out and notifies the client |\n| Agent initiated | The agent decides that contact or intervention is needed |\n\n5.4.2 Contact takes place at three points in the day: the Morning Plan, during the day, and Daily Close. Daily Close is one of three kinds of daily contact.\n\n5.4.3 **Morning Plan.** An optional message at the start of the day. It states the day's commitments, their timing, and each item that needs the client's attention.\n\n5.4.4 **During the day.** Four kinds of message: a reminder before a commitment, an update when a Plan or the schedule changes, a notice that an action was carried out, and a follow up when further work needs the client.\n\n5.4.5 **Daily Close** is the daily contact in which the agent and the client review what happened, address the remaining work, and confirm the next day's Plan. It is the regular contact.\n\n5.4.6 Daily Close opens with the first question in 6.5. The agent then asks about a commitment only where 7.15 applies.\n\n5.4.7 The agent asks about the commitment by name. The client is never asked to reconstruct the whole day from memory.\n\n5.4.8 The client does not have to wait for Daily Close. The client can report a new commitment, a change, or an outcome at any time, and it is filed the same way.\n\n5.4.9 The client chooses how much contact they receive and when. The client can turn on the Morning Plan, reminders, updates, and notices separately.\n\n5.4.10 Contact is managed by five factors: the client's contact preferences, the timing and importance of the commitment, the consequences and minimums on file for it, whether information, a decision, or an action is needed from the client, and whether an authorized action was carried out and the client asked to be told.\n\n5.4.11 A message states information the client can use. A message asks for a reply only when information, a decision, or an action is needed from the client. Routine authorized work proceeds with no reply.\n\n5.4.12 **Sender.** A message the agent wrote or released is shown as from the agent. A message that software sent under a standing permission is shown as an automated Universe City message. Software never signs as the agent.\n\n5.4.13 The contact settings, the delivery methods, and the scheduling rules are specified in Folder 01 Time Operations.\n\n### 5.5 Follow through\n\n5.5.1 When the client reports an executed commitment, four steps run in order.\n\n5.5.2 One. The commitment is marked executed, with the time and each consequence the client reported.\n\n5.5.3 Two. The Follow through condition, the linked ongoing work, and the requirements of each folder the commitment is linked to are checked.\n\n5.5.4 Three. Each piece of remaining work a standing permission covers is carried out and filed. No reply is requested from the client. Each other piece is prepared for the agent.\n\n5.5.5 Four. A follow up is opened for each piece of information, each decision, and each permission that is still missing.\n\n5.5.6 The client is asked for four kinds of input only: missing information, a decision that belongs to the client, a permission, and an action only the client can do.\n\n5.5.7 No work is created to make the client interact. A receipt, an amount, or an itemized list is requested only when the folder that receives it needs it.\n\n### 5.6 Authorization\n\n5.6.1 Three acts are kept separate: preparation, authorized execution, and approval of a permanent change to the client's file. One never stands in for another.\n\n5.6.2 **Preparation.** The agent or software drafts an update, a schedule change, or a message. No entry on file changes. Preparation needs no permission.\n\n5.6.3 **Authorized execution.** A **standing permission** is a permission the client gave in advance for a named kind of routine action. An action it covers is carried out with no further approval. Software can carry it out with no person releasing it.\n\n5.6.4 An action carried out under a standing permission is filed with who carried it out and which permission covered it.\n\n5.6.5 **Approval of a permanent change.** An entry that states what the client directly reported is filed as the client's statement. An entry that required interpretation is a proposal. A proposal is filed only under the proposal and approval rules in `ARCHITECTURE.md`. No standing permission covers a proposal. Software never approves one.\n\n5.6.6 A person is required in four cases: the permission does not clearly cover the action, judgment is needed beyond the established rule, the action would change the client's file or obligations in a way that requires interpretation, or the client has asked for review by a person.\n\n5.6.7 The client's decision is required in six cases: a commitment the client has not agreed to, two commitments that compete for the same time, a change to a minimum in Standards, a change that cannot be reversed, a record to be voided, and a message to be sent on the client's behalf. Language Standard 10.9.1.\n\n5.6.8 Each standing permission and the actions it covers are specified in Folder 01 Time Operations.\n\n### 5.7 One case, grocery shopping\n\n5.7.1 The client reports that the grocery shopping happened.\n\n5.7.2 The shopping commitment is marked executed.\n\n5.7.3 Where a purchase amount is available and Finance uses it, the Finance entry is prepared.\n\n5.7.4 Where the items are known and Inventory maintains them, the Inventory changes are prepared.\n\n5.7.5 The remaining work is identified. The part a standing permission covers is placed on the schedule.\n\n5.7.6 Each unresolved follow up stays open until its requirement is met.\n\n5.7.7 Steps 5.7.2 to 5.7.6 are done by the agent and by software. The client takes part only where information, a permission, a decision, or an action only the client can do is required. The shopping commitment stays executed while linked work is open.\n\n### 5.8 The schedule by hand\n\n5.8.1 The client can open the schedule and work on it directly. Blocks can be dragged, resized, added, edited, and deleted. Every change is kept on file and the agent can see what changed.\n\n### 5.9 Order and frequency\n\n5.9.1 The Version 3 rule that set the order of work by lowest position first is retired.\n\n5.9.2 The agent selects the next question by four factors: the information still missing, the importance of the commitment to the client, the consequences on file, and the client's preferences.\n\n5.9.3 The Version 3 rule that set how often the agent asks by the twelve answers is retired. How often the client is contacted is governed by 5.4.\n\n5.9.4 Start, Target, and Position for Time still come from the twelve questions. Position does not set the order of work in Time.\n\n## 6. The agent's questions\n\n6.1 Every question has a purpose before it is asked. The purpose is one missing piece: a block, a condition of a commitment, an outcome, a consequence, a decision, or a permission. A question that names no missing piece is not asked.\n\n6.2 The agent asks one question at a time. The agent does not ask for information already on file.\n\n6.3 The wording below is what the agent uses. The questions use grocery shopping as the subject. The agent substitutes the actual subject every time.\n\n### 6.4 Making a Plan\n\n> What already has to happen tomorrow?\n>\n> Is there a part of tomorrow you want to be different from usual?\n>\n> Here is what I have for tomorrow. Is that still the plan?\n>\n> How realistic does this plan feel to you?\n>\n> What time tomorrow do you want to talk about how the day went?\n\n### 6.5 Checking an outcome\n\n> Did today go as planned?\n>\n> Did the grocery shopping happen?\n>\n> Is the grocery shopping finished?\n>\n> What time did the grocery shopping happen?\n>\n> Did the later time affect another commitment?\n>\n> Is the grocery shopping still needed?\n\n### 6.6 Establishing a condition\n\n> What time does the grocery shopping need to happen?\n>\n> How late can the grocery shopping move?\n>\n> What has to happen for the grocery shopping to count as done?\n>\n> What does it affect if the grocery shopping moves or does not happen?\n>\n> Does one of your minimums apply to the grocery shopping?\n>\n> What is left to do after the grocery shopping?\n\n### 6.7 Asking for a decision or a permission\n\n> The grocery shopping and your appointment are both at 5:00 PM. Which one holds?\n>\n> Do you want me to move the grocery shopping to tomorrow at 10:00 AM?\n>\n> Do you want me to put the remaining work on your schedule when the time is already known?\n\n### 6.8 Setting contact\n\n> Do you want a message each morning with the plan for the day?\n>\n> Do you want a reminder before the grocery shopping?\n>\n> Do you want a notice each time I update your schedule?\n\n### 6.9 Collecting a block\n\n6.9.1 These questions are used when a block is missing. They are not asked as a fixed sequence.\n\n> What time did you wake up?\n>\n> How long did getting ready take you this morning?\n>\n> What time did you leave the house?\n>\n> What time did you actually get to work?\n>\n> What time did your shift end?\n>\n> What time did you get home?\n>\n> How long was it before you started your next activity?\n\n### 6.10 Establishing the kind of block\n\n> Is that shift paid?\n>\n> Was that required by the job, or did you choose to do it?\n>\n> Was that part of the shift, or separate from it?\n\n### 6.11 Confidence\n\n> Is that what actually happened yesterday, or roughly what usually happens?\n>\n> Do you know the actual time, or is that an estimate?\n\n### 6.12 Filling a gap\n\n> What were you doing between 2:00 PM and 5:00 PM?\n>\n> There is nothing on file for Sunday. What were you doing?\n\n### 6.13 Asked once per new kind of work\n\n> Is that work you get paid for, or your own?\n>\n> How long does getting ready usually take you?\n\n### 6.14 Rules for every question\n\n6.14.1 Every question names the subject it is asking about. The agent never uses a pronoun where the subject belongs. How long does getting ready usually take you is a question. How long does that usually take you is not ready.\n\n6.14.2 A question that could be answered about more than one subject is not ready. What time did you finish is not a question. What time did your shift end is.\n\n6.14.3 The agent treats a client's direct statement as a statement. The agent does not repeat it back and ask whether the client meant it. The agent asks for clarification in two cases: the answer is ambiguous, or a consequential change to the file depends on it.\n\n6.14.4 Every time in a question is written with AM or PM.\n\n6.14.5 The agent does not ask what time it is or what day it is. The current date and time are always available to it.\n\n6.14.6 The agent speaks times as 7:00 AM. It never uses twenty four hour time.\n\n## 7. Triggers\n\n7.1 The conditions under which a subject is raised that the client did not bring up.\n\n7.2 No conversation is opened for work that needs no input from the client. An action a standing permission covers is carried out and filed. The client is notified where the client's contact preferences ask for a notice.\n\n7.3 A commitment is reported executed. The four steps in 5.5 run.\n\n7.4 A commitment did not happen or was partly executed. The agent asks whether the remaining part is still required. The agent keeps it, moves it, or removes it as the client decides.\n\n7.5 A commitment is executed and its follow through is open. The work a standing permission covers is scheduled with no question to the client. The agent asks only for a missing piece.\n\n7.6 A recurring responsibility comes due. The next commitment is placed on the schedule under the recurrence the client stated. No question is asked.\n\n7.7 A commitment is added that overlaps an existing block. The agent states the conflict and asks which one holds.\n\n7.8 A change affects a commitment that has a stated consequence or an applicable minimum. The agent states the effect and asks the client to decide.\n\n7.9 A commitment is added with no travel attached. The agent asks how the client is getting there and back.\n\n7.10 A block is edited by hand. The agent can see the change and may ask about it. It does not treat the change as a correction to be logged separately.\n\n7.11 A day passes with no entry on file. The agent asks about that day only where an open requirement depends on it. The three requirements are a commitment on that day's Plan with no outcome, an open follow up, and a block the schedule is still missing. The agent asks once, at the next contact. Where no requirement is open, the agent does not ask.\n\n7.12 An estimate has stood for more than a week. The agent asks whether the client now knows the actual figure.\n\n7.13 A commitment is added that takes the controlled hours figure below the client's stated target. The agent states the figure before and after. It does not advise.\n\n7.14 A block the client claimed for their own work is repeatedly given to another commitment. The agent states how many times it has happened.\n\n7.15 The agent does not ask about every commitment on a Plan. A planned commitment gets a question at Daily Close when one of these seven is true: it is new, the client marked it important, its timing is uncertain, it has differed from the Plan more than once, another commitment depends on it, the client asked for accountability on it, or it is a change from the client's usual week.\n\n7.16 The seven conditions rest on five factors: how uncertain the commitment is, how important it is to the client, the consequences on file for it, the outcomes already on file, and the client's preference.\n\n7.17 The agent asks less about a commitment as its outcomes keep matching the Plan. No fixed count of matching outcomes applies. The criteria are specified in Folder 01 Time Operations.\n\n## 8. Folder limits\n\n8.1 These apply in addition to section 10 of the Language Standard.\n\n8.2 The agent does not comment on how the client spends their time. It files the hours and the outcomes.\n\n8.3 The agent does not tell a client they are working too much, sleeping too little, or spending too long on a subject.\n\n8.4 The agent does not interpret sleep, fatigue, or a condition the client describes as a medical problem. Those are filed as hours and routed to Health.\n\n8.5 The agent does not calculate a real hourly rate. That is Salary, and it depends on this folder.\n\n8.6 The agent does not fill a gap in the record with a typical day. An unaccounted hour stays unaccounted until the client says otherwise.\n\n8.7 **Client authority.** The client decides what goes on the Plan, what each commitment requires, and what changes. The agent does not remove a commitment, change a condition, or set a minimum without the client's decision. The client can edit the schedule at any time.\n\n8.8 **Authorized action.** The agent and software take only the actions the client has permitted. A standing permission never covers a record to be voided, a message sent on the client's behalf, or a change the client cannot reverse. Each of the three needs the client's confirmation. Language Standard 10.9.1.\n\n8.9 **Evidence.** A source proves only what it directly proves. A planned block does not prove the commitment happened. A bank transaction does not prove why the money was spent. Silence does not prove a commitment was missed.\n\n8.10 The agent does not invent a consequence, an amount, a purchase, or a completion.\n\n8.11 The agent does not treat a changed or missed commitment as a failure. The outcome is filed and the next Plan uses it. The agent does not comment on the client's reliability or effort. Language Standard 10.6.4.\n\n## 9. The arc\n\n9.1 Three stages: intake, analyze and adapt, system maintenance. Language Standard 3.7 and 8.2.11. The four stage arc of Version 3 is superseded.\n\n9.2 **Before the arc.** Enrollment establishes the client's initial information and the Start and Target for Time. Orientation follows in the Resident Portal. The client does not build the schedule during Enrollment. Initial Time work starts after Orientation. The order is Application, email confirmation, Enrollment, Orientation, Time. The free first day of Version 3 is retired.\n\n9.3 **One, intake.** The agent establishes what already has the client's time, assembles the first seven day schedule, and makes the first Plan with the client. The first Plan includes the realism check in 5.3.3 and the time of Daily Close in 5.3.4. The Resident Portal home opens when the first seven day schedule is on file and the client has confirmed the first Plan. A completed week of activity is not required.\n\n9.3.1 At the start of Time intake, after Orientation, the calendar is available even if the schedule is not yet established. The agent fills it from existing File information and new conversation as timing becomes established and authorized. An unfilled portion does not prevent the client from seeing or using the calendar. The conditions in 9.3 and section 10 still govern when the initial system is operational; calendar visibility does not certify that those conditions have been met.\n\n9.4 **Two, analyze and adapt.** The client carries out the Plan. Outcomes are checked at Daily Close, follow through is carried out under 5.5, and the agent compares each Plan with what happened. Where the same outcome repeats, the agent proposes a change to the next Plan or to the schedule. The client decides.\n\n9.5 **Three, system maintenance.** The agent asks fewer questions as commitments become established. Contact follows the client's preferences in 5.4. The agent asks about what is missing, what changed, and the commitments named in 7.15. The schedule receives commitments from every folder. The Review between the client and the agent uses the outcomes on file.\n\n## 10. Operational conditions\n\n10.1 The folder is operational when all three are true. The folder is never complete. It is established, maintained, and corrected.\n\n10.2 A seven day schedule is on file. It holds the client's recurring commitments, with their travel and preparation filed as costs of them.\n\n10.3 The client has confirmed at least one Plan.\n\n10.4 The time of Daily Close is on the schedule.\n\n10.5 Two further facts show the system is in use: a completed Daily Close with outcomes on file, and a decision the client answered with the schedule. Neither one is a condition.\n\n10.6 The hours the client controls and the longest unbroken block are figures the schedule produces. Neither one is a condition.\n\n10.7 No follow up is required for the folder to be operational. A client whose commitments produce no further work meets the three conditions.\n\n10.8 The grade measures the work done during the month. It does not measure the target chosen or whether the target was reached.\n\n## 11. The system\n\n11.1 A seven day schedule.\n\n11.2 The schedule is the working system. Eight parts run on it.\n\n| Part | What it is | How it connects |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n| Schedule | The recurring structure of the client's week | The source of each day's Plan. Changed when repeated outcomes show the week runs differently |\n| Plan | The commitments the client intends to execute on one day | Taken from the schedule, plus what is different that day. Confirmed by the client |\n| Commitment | A specific action the client intends to execute | Sits on a Plan, in a block. Carries the five conditions and its outcome |\n| Follow up | Information, a confirmation, or an action still required | Linked to the conversation or commitment it came from. Open until its requirement is met |\n| Ongoing work | A continuing responsibility | Holds its commitments and follow ups. Active through each recurrence |\n| Morning Plan | An optional message at the start of the day | Taken from the day's Plan. Sent where the client turned it on |\n| Daily Close | The regular daily contact on the day's Plan | Puts the outcome of each commitment on file. Confirms the next Plan |\n| Review | The meeting between the client and the agent | Uses the outcomes on file to decide changes to the schedule |\n\n11.3 One commitment can produce more than one follow up. One piece of ongoing work can hold more than one commitment. One action can affect more than one folder. Each link is kept on file, and each change of state is kept on file.\n\n11.4 The schedule is also the client's calendar. Appointments, reminders, and dates from every other folder are placed in it. Time owns the calendar for the whole program.\n\n11.4.1 The calendar is the visible working surface of the schedule. Its day and week views show timed commitments and blocks in time slots. Its month view shows them on their dates. The client can open an entry to see the maintained information, what remains unknown, and its history.\n\n11.4.2 A change established through conversation appears in the calendar. A permitted direct change in the calendar updates the same File entry. The views do not independently own or duplicate the record. They must respect the resident's decisions, authorization, original statements, earlier versions, and links to other folders.\n\n11.5 The schedule produces two figures once a week of observed time is on file: the hours the client controls, and the longest unbroken block.\n\n### 11.6 Successful execution\n\n11.6.1 Two results are kept apart: executed, and confirmed successful.\n\n11.6.2 A commitment is executed when the Completion condition the client stated is met.\n\n11.6.3 Execution is confirmed successful when the commitment is executed and Consequences is none confirmed. Confirmed successful is read from those two fields. It is not a fifth outcome field.\n\n11.6.4 Where Consequences is not established, the commitment is executed and success is not confirmed. Not established is never counted as no harm.\n\n11.6.5 A changed time is filed whenever the actual time differs from the Plan. A changed time alone is never a failure.\n\n11.6.6 A partly executed commitment stays on file as partly executed. It is never filed as executed.\n\n11.6.7 An alternative agreed in advance is executed when it meets the Completion condition. It is confirmed successful on the same terms as every other commitment.\n\n11.6.8 One case. A client plans to buy groceries at 5:00 PM.\n\n| What happened | Execution | Timing | Consequences | Result |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- | :-- | :-- |\n| Shopping happened at 5:00 PM | Executed | As planned | Not established | Executed. Success not confirmed |\n| Shopping happened at 7:00 PM. The client said no other commitment was affected | Executed | Changed | None confirmed | Confirmed successful |\n| Shopping happened at 7:00 PM. The client missed an appointment | Executed | Changed | Reported | Executed, with one consequence on file |\n| Shopping was started and was not finished | Partly executed | Not established | Not established | Partly executed |\n| Shopping did not happen | Did not happen | Does not apply | Not established | Did not happen |\n\n11.6.9 In row three the agent raises the consequence at the next Review.\n\n11.6.10 In rows four and five the agent asks whether the remaining shopping is still required, then keeps or revises the Plan as the client decides.\n\n11.6.11 The agent does not ask about consequences only to confirm success. The agent asks where the client stated a consequence in advance, or where the timing changed and the new time overlaps a second commitment. In every other case the commitment stays executed with Consequences not established.\n\n11.7 How the system runs is specified in Folder 01 Time Operations.\n\n## 12. What the agent can answer\n\n12.1 Once this folder exists the agent can answer the following. This list is the engineering requirement for the folder.\n\n12.2 Did today go as planned?\n\n12.3 What did I plan this week, and what happened to each commitment?\n\n12.4 What is still open from the commitments I already executed?\n\n12.5 What is coming up, and what does each commitment need from me?\n\n12.6 What does it affect if I move this commitment?\n\n12.7 Which follow ups are mine, and which are my agent's?\n\n12.8 What do my other folders have on my schedule this week?\n\n12.9 Which commitments keep changing, and what changed them?\n\n12.10 How many hours do I control this week, and where do they sit?\n\n12.11 What is my longest unbroken block, and which day is it on?\n\n12.12 Can I take this shift and still keep the next day's commitments?\n\n12.13 When can I put this appointment?\n\n12.14 What did that commitment really cost me once travel and recovery are counted?\n\n12.15 Which day of mine is the most overloaded?\n\n12.16 How many hours of last week are still unaccounted for?\n\n12.17 How long does getting ready actually take me, as opposed to what I assume?\n\n## 13. Connections\n\n13.1 Serves every folder. Appointments and reminders from every folder are placed in this folder's calendar. An action from another folder that has a time, a deadline, or a recurrence is scheduled here as a commitment.\n\n13.2 Coordinates with Inventory. A shopping commitment executed here produces Inventory changes where the items are known and Inventory maintains them. Work that Inventory produces with a date is scheduled here.\n\n13.3 Feeds Salary. A real hourly rate cannot be calculated until unpaid travel, preparation, and recovery are counted against pay. Salary depends on this folder.\n\n13.4 Fed by Standards. The minimums that apply to a commitment come from Standards.\n\n13.5 Feeds Standards. A decision is checked against both what the client will accept and whether the time exists.\n\n13.6 Feeds Discernment. A commitment that took the controlled hours figure below target is visible here and raised there.\n\n13.7 Feeds Equilibrium. Where the client's hours actually go is one half of the comparison against what the client said matters.\n\n13.8 Fed by Career Development, Schedule. The work schedule supplies planned shifts. This folder holds what was worked.\n\n13.9 Feeds Finance. A purchase amount from an executed commitment is filed in Finance.\n\n13.10 Read by Integrity. Integrity reads the commitments and outcomes on file here. It covers every commitment, at work and outside work, and holds no second copy.\n\n## 14. Build status\n\n14.1 The status below describes the repository on October 11, 2026.\n\n### 14.2 Built\n\n14.2.1 Blocks are stored in the database. Each block holds a date, a start, an end, a label, a kind, a status, paid or unpaid, cost of, and who wrote it.\n\n14.2.2 Every correction to a block is kept with the earlier version. That history cannot be edited or deleted.\n\n14.2.3 The existing numerical week summary in the Resident Portal. One row for each day, with four figures: observed hours, estimated hours, unaccounted hours, and the hours that were the client's. This summary is not the required time-positioned weekly calendar.\n\n14.2.4 The hours that were the client's are calculated from observed blocks only, as 3.6.3 requires.\n\n14.2.5 The first Plan. It asks what already has to happen tomorrow, what the client wants to do differently, how realistic the Plan feels, how the agent follows up, and what time Daily Close goes on the Plan. It saves the Plan as blocks.\n\n14.2.6 The field that Version 3 item 15.2 asked to rename is named `cost_of` in the code. That item is closed.\n\n### 14.3 Listed as live in Version 3 and absent from the repository\n\n14.3.1 Plain language collection, the day view by whole hours, the month view, direct editing by hand, and reminders. Each of the five is to build.\n\n14.3.2 A true weekly time-grid calendar, with dated and timed entries positioned in the hours where they belong, is also to build. The numerical summary in 14.2.3 is not evidence that this visual weekly calendar is built. Day, week, and month must be checked together against the underlying Time records before any is marked operational.\n\n### 14.4 To build\n\n14.4.1 A place for the conversation, with each message linked to the folders it concerns.\n\n14.4.2 Commitments, with the five conditions and the four outcome fields.\n\n14.4.3 Follow ups and ongoing work, with their links and their history.\n\n14.4.4 Daily Close, the Morning Plan, the messages during the day, and the client's contact preferences.\n\n14.4.5 Standing permissions, authorized execution by software, and a path for software to file a proposal.\n\n14.4.6 The questions in section 12, answered in plain language.\n\n14.4.7 The longest unbroken block.\n\n14.4.8 Reminders that arrive when the app is closed.\n\n### 14.5 Out of step\n\n14.5.1 The code holds five block statuses: observed, required, planned, suggested, and estimated. Section 3.2 defines observed or estimated, and planned or done. The reconciliation is assigned to Folder 01 Time Operations, part 2.1.\n\n## 15. Open items\n\n15.1 Curriculum. A revised **Folder 01 Time Curriculum Version 2**, dated October 11, 2026, has been located in Google Drive (document titled `Folder 01 Time Curriculum (4)`). It contains twelve questions about commitments, planning, execution, changes, review, follow through, and support; section 18 moves the earlier time-use subjects into Schedule intake. The document is not yet an approved repository copy. Its existing client copy is preserved. Reconcile its section 18 with the calendar presentation in 2.9.1, 9.3.1, and 11.4.1 without changing the approved sequence or treating lessons as a condition for using the schedule.\n\n15.2 Research. An updated **Folder 01 Time Research**, with entries dated October 11, 2026, has been located in Google Drive (document titled `Folder 01 Time Research (3)`). It includes newer analysis supporting and qualifying the planning and monitoring approach. It is not yet in the repository. The visual calendar requirement is a product/implementation decision; do not claim the research independently validates this exact interface. Preserve the dated evidence and qualifications when reconciling the Research with this specification.\n\n15.3 Every other item that Version 4 first listed as open is resolved in this document or assigned to Folder 01 Time Operations. Folder 01 Time Developments holds the list, dated October 11, 2026.\n\nGoverned by `LANGUAGE_STANDARD.md`, section 8, for folder composition.\n"},{"key":"operations","title":"Folder 01 Time Operations","path":"docs/systems/TIME_OPERATIONS.md","text":"# Folder 01 Time Operations\n\nDepartment I, Agency Assessment. Systems operations. Version 1.1, October 11, 2026. How the Time system runs: fields, states, views, queries, permissions, and contact.\n\n## 1. About this document\n\n1.1 This is one of five documents for this folder. `docs/systems/TIME.md` states what the folder is. This document states how the system runs. It is read by a developer.\n\n1.2 A version of this document was written on August 29, 2026. That version was never filed. This file is a new document. It does not reconstruct the August 29 version.\n\n1.3 Each part below carries one of two marks. Defined means `TIME.md` states it or the repository already holds it. To define means no decision is on file.\n\n1.4 No implementation shall assume an answer to a part marked To define.\n\n1.5 Where this document and `TIME.md` disagree, `TIME.md` governs. `LANGUAGE_STANDARD.md` governs the wording of both.\n\n1.6 A change to this document is entered in `docs/systems/TIME_DEVELOPMENTS.md` with the date and the reason.\n\n1.7 Version 1.1 specifies the calendar-first views and their record contract as established requirements. A view or interaction whose implementation mechanics are still undecided remains marked To define. This revision does not assert that the founder-test calendar has passed verification.\n\n## 2. Fields\n\n### 2.1 The block. Defined and built.\n\n2.1.1 The `blocks` table holds one row for each block. Migration `0001_init.sql` created it.\n\n| Field | Permitted values | Rule |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n| `date` | A calendar date | The day the block starts on |\n| `starts_at` | A local time, as hours and minutes | Kept as local time in the client's timezone |\n| `ends_at` | A local time, or empty | A value past midnight belongs to the day the block started on |\n| `label` | Text | Required |\n| `kind` | One of eleven kinds | Listed in 2.1.2 |\n| `status` | One of five statuses | Listed in 2.1.3 |\n| `paid` | paid, unpaid, none | `TIME.md` 3.2.3 |\n| `cost_of` | Another block, or empty | `TIME.md` 3.2.4 |\n| `source` | resident, agent, ai | Who wrote the row. The database sets it. The writer cannot choose it |\n| `note` | Text, or empty | The first Plan keeps its answers here |\n\n2.1.2 The eleven kinds in the code: sleep, getting ready, travel, paid work, unpaid work time, household time, caring for people, appointments, time with people, rest and leisure, and time on what you are building.\n\n2.1.3 The five statuses in the code: observed, required, planned, suggested, and estimated. `TIME.md` section 3.2 holds two pairs of values: observed or estimated, and planned or done.\n\n2.1.4 To define: one set of block statuses for the specification and the code. `TIME.md` 3.6.1 files every future period as estimated. The code holds required, planned, and suggested for a future block. The reconciliation was assigned to this document on October 11, 2026. Neither set changes until it is decided.\n\n### 2.2 The commitment. Defined and not built.\n\n2.2.1 `TIME.md` 3.3 states the fields: a label, the block the commitment is planned in, the five conditions, and the four outcome fields.\n\n| Outcome field | Permitted values |\n| :-- | :-- |\n| Execution | planned, executed, partly executed, did not happen |\n| Timing | as planned, changed, not established |\n| Consequences | reported, none confirmed, not established |\n| Alternative | used or not used, and which alternative |\n\n2.2.2 To define: the table that holds a commitment, the form each of the five conditions is stored in, and the link from a commitment to its block.\n\n### 2.3 The follow up. Defined and not built.\n\n2.3.1 `TIME.md` 3.4 states the four fields: the owner, the next action, the conversation or commitment the follow up came from, and a status of open or closed.\n\n2.3.2 To define: the table that holds a follow up.\n\n### 2.4 Ongoing work. Defined and not built.\n\n2.4.1 `TIME.md` 3.5 states the fields: a label, the recurrence the client stated, and a link to each commitment and follow up inside it.\n\n2.4.2 To define: the table that holds ongoing work, and the form a recurrence is stored in.\n\n### 2.5 The Plan. Defined and built in part.\n\n2.5.1 `TIME.md` 5.3.1 defines the Plan. The first Plan is built. It saves each planned commitment as a block with the status planned.\n\n2.5.2 To define: whether a Plan is stored as its own entry, and how a confirmed Plan is told apart from an unconfirmed one.\n\n## 3. States and transitions\n\n3.1 Block correction. Defined and built. A change to a block writes the earlier version to `block_revisions`. A row in `block_revisions` cannot be edited or deleted.\n\n3.2 Execution. Defined in `TIME.md` 3.3.6 and 3.6.6. A commitment starts as planned. It moves to executed, partly executed, or did not happen when the client reports the outcome or evidence on file proves it.\n\n3.3 To define: whether a partly executed commitment can later move to executed, and what is filed when it does.\n\n3.4 Follow up. Defined in `TIME.md` 5.7.6. A follow up is open until its requirement is met. Then it is closed.\n\n3.5 Proposal. Defined and built. A proposal holds one of five states: proposed, confirmed, rejected, superseded, or edited. `ARCHITECTURE.md` states who can move a proposal from one state to the next.\n\n3.6 To define: the path by which software files a proposal. Today only an agent can create one.\n\n## 4. Views\n\n4.1 The existing **numerical weekly summary**. Defined and built in the repository described by `TIME.md` 14.2.3. It shows one row per day and four figures: observed hours, estimated hours, unaccounted hours, and hours the client controls. It is **not** the visual week calendar required by `TIME.md` 2.9.1 and 11.4.1.\n\n4.2 The first Plan. Defined and built. It asks five questions in order. `TIME.md` 14.2.5 lists them.\n\n4.3 **Calendar requirement defined, implementation to build.** Day, week, and month are views of the same Time system. They must show the actual dates and, in day and week, visually positioned time slots and blocks. Direct editing by hand is established in `TIME.md` 5.8.1. The exact UI event rules, accessibility interactions, rendering granularity, and wiring to existing storage remain to define and test. The Plan with outcomes and the open follow-up list remain to define as views.\n\n4.4 To define: the agent console view of commitments, follow ups, ongoing work, and proposals for one client.\n\n4.5 **First Time surface. Defined in `TIME.md` 2.4.1, 9.3.1 and 11.4.** The calendar is available when Time intake begins after Orientation, before the initial seven-day schedule or first Plan is complete. It can initially be sparse. Entries appear as commitments are established from the File and conversation. Visibility of a calendar is not proof that Time's operational conditions have been satisfied.\n\n4.6 **Day view. Product behavior defined; build status unverified.** Show the selected calendar date with the actual time axis and timed entries occupying their proper times. Show untimed, unknown-time, or incomplete commitments distinctly rather than inventing a start, end, or duration. Select an entry to open its source-backed details and history.\n\n4.7 **Week view. Product behavior defined; visual grid not yet verified.** Show seven consecutive calendar days with the corresponding time axis and blocks positioned in their actual local hours. Do not substitute the numerical table of 4.1 for the visual week. The numerical figures can coexist without replacing it.\n\n4.8 **Month view. Product behavior defined; build status unverified.** Show an actual calendar month with weekday alignment and commitments under the correct dates. The month view may summarize multiple entries per date; selecting an entry opens its maintained details. The month view reads the same record as day and week.\n\n4.9 **One maintained schedule. Defined in `TIME.md` 2.9.1 and 11.4.2.** Conversation, day/week/month, the selected File view, and Activity refer to the same authorized entries and history. Rendering is a view of the data, not a second store or a new copy of the commitment. Repeated approval of the same action shall not be reported as a fresh change; the proposed, confirmed, unchanged, and revised outcomes remain distinguishable.\n\n4.10 **Unknown and tentative time. Defined in `TIME.md` 2.9.2 and 3.6.** A date with unknown timing remains a dated or unscheduled entry as permitted by the existing record model. A known start with unknown end is never drawn as an invented duration. A tentative time is distinguishable from a confirmed one, and does not become confirmed without applicable authority. A view must not silently transform an incomplete commitment into a fabricated block.\n\n4.11 **Client interaction. Defined by `TIME.md` 2.9, 5.8 and 8.7; detailed mechanics to define.** The resident can open the calendar and add, move, resize, edit, or remove authorized time blocks directly. Each permitted operation must validate the affected record, retain the source and prior versions, and be visible to the guide. Editing via the visual grid never bypasses File approval or scheduling permissions. On denial or conflict, explain the result without claiming a change.\n\n4.12 **Overnight time and timezone. Existing rule and remaining implementation detail.** `TIME_OPERATIONS.md` 2.1.1 already assigns a block to its start date and uses local time for the client's timezone. To define and test: rendering across midnight, daylight-saving changes, ranges spanning a date boundary, conversion between local time and stored values, and how incomplete endings appear in all views. Do not assume a 9-to-5 day or truncate a late shift at midnight.\n\n4.13 **Calendar progression. Defined by `TIME.md` 9.3, 10.2–10.4.** The guide progressively establishes the first seven-day schedule and first confirmed Plan. Calendar entries must become visible once authorized, without asking the client to fill the grid or complete all the teaching lessons. The resident's assessment answers are collected during Enrollment as separately established; calendar-first Time intake does not reverse Enrollment and Orientation.\n\n4.14 **To define and verify:** precise axis/grid resolution; keyboard, touch and screen-reader interaction; collision/overlap handling; recurrences in day/week/month; all-day and deadline display; local-time and DST edge cases; status legends; which service queries power each view; direct edit authorization and rollback; matching stored blocks/commitments with the founder-test data model; and responsive behavior. Those decisions belong to implementation review rather than being silently assumed by a visual mockup.\n\n4.15 **Acceptance test for the system.** Starting from a sparse calendar, establish an irregular work week in conversation. After the resident confirms an October 16 shift at 3:00 PM, show it in the correct place in day, week, and month without inventing its end. Open the record and verify source and history. Change the timing under the permitted process and verify all views agree, no duplicate action is filed, and direct calendar edits update the same record. Repeat with an overnight shift and a deferred estimate. A static grid or a page listing facts does not pass.\n\n## 5. Queries\n\n5.1 `TIME.md` section 12 lists seventeen questions. Each question needs one query. `LANGUAGE_STANDARD.md` 8.4.4 requires the two lists to match.\n\n5.2 The week view already calculates the figures behind `TIME.md` 12.10 and 12.16. No query answers a question in plain language yet.\n\n5.3 To define: the data each of the seventeen queries reads.\n\n5.4 **Calendar read contract. Defined requirement; query implementation to verify.** Day, week and month resolve the same canonical Time blocks, scheduled commitments, applicable recurrence and permitted status data for the selected date range. The query layer must retain event identity, time zone, uncertainty, status, origin/source link, and earlier versions as needed for detail/history. It must not infer a time from business opening hours or render an unconfirmed estimate as a confirmed commitment. Exact endpoints and indexes remain to define.\n\n## 6. Action permissions\n\n6.1 Defined in `TIME.md` 5.6. Three acts are kept separate: preparation, authorized execution, and approval of a permanent change to the client's file.\n\n6.1.1 A calendar drag, resize, add or deletion is an action under the same permission and authority rules as an equivalent conversational change. The interface cannot assert that dragging alone authorizes a protected change. A repeated confirmation of a completed pending action does not cause another mutation or misreport an unchanged value as Changed. The implementation must preserve a meaningful, idempotent decision outcome.\n\n6.2 Defined. Software can carry out an action a standing permission covers, with no person releasing it. The action is filed with who carried it out and which permission covered it.\n\n6.3 Defined. A person is required in the four cases in `TIME.md` 5.6.6. The client's decision is required in the six cases in `TIME.md` 5.6.7.\n\n6.4 Four routine actions were named when the rule was set on October 11, 2026: keeping a recurrence the client agreed to, placing established work on the schedule in available time, updating the schedule when a commitment changes, and notifying the client.\n\n6.5 To define: the full list of standing permissions and the actions each one covers.\n\n6.6 To define: the words the client reads when granting a standing permission, and how the client withdraws one.\n\n6.7 To define: what counts as available time when work is placed on the schedule.\n\n6.8 To define: how a person is brought in for the four cases in `TIME.md` 5.6.6, and what the client sees while that person decides.\n\n6.9 To define: retry rules for an automatic action that fails, and what is filed when it fails.\n\n## 7. Contact\n\n7.1 Defined in `TIME.md` 5.4. Contact has three origins: client requested, permitted automatic, and agent initiated.\n\n7.2 Defined. Contact takes place at three points in the day: the Morning Plan, during the day, and Daily Close. Four kinds of message are sent during the day.\n\n7.3 Defined. The client chooses how much contact they receive and when. Five factors govern each contact. `TIME.md` 5.4.10 lists them.\n\n7.4 Defined. A message that software sent under a standing permission is shown as an automated Universe City message. Software never signs as the agent.\n\n7.5 Built in part. The first Plan asks how the agent follows up and offers four choices: none, before, after, and both. It also asks what time Daily Close goes on the Plan. Both answers are saved with the blocks of that Plan.\n\n7.6 To define: the full set of contact settings and the setting a new client starts with.\n\n7.7 To define: the delivery methods. No Time message is built today.\n\n7.8 To define: the timing rules for the Morning Plan and for a reminder before a commitment.\n\n7.9 To define: the hours in which no message is sent.\n\n7.10 To define: the exact label the client sees on an automated message.\n\n## 8. Asking less\n\n8.1 Defined in `TIME.md` 7.15 to 7.17. A planned commitment gets a question when one of seven conditions is true. The seven rest on five factors. No fixed count of matching outcomes applies.\n\n8.2 To define: the criteria by which a commitment is treated as established.\n\n8.3 To define: what returns an established commitment to regular questions.\n\n8.4 Defined in `TIME.md` 5.9. The agent selects the next question by four factors: the information still missing, the importance of the commitment to the client, the consequences on file, and the client's preferences. The Version 3 rule of lowest position first is retired.\n\n8.5 To define: how the four factors are weighed when two questions are both ready.\n\n## 9. Further items to define\n\n9.1 What is filed when a client reports a purchase amount while Finance is not operational.\n\n9.2 How Integrity reads commitments and outcomes from Time. `TIME.md` 4.4.7 rules that Integrity holds no second copy.\n\n9.3 The rule for two commitments that compete for the same time, after the client has decided which one holds.\n\n9.4 The fields of a Morning Plan message.\n\nGoverned by `LANGUAGE_STANDARD.md`, section 8.4, for composition.\n"},{"key":"developments","title":"Folder 01 Time Developments","path":"docs/systems/TIME_DEVELOPMENTS.md","text":"# Folder 01 Time Developments\n\nDepartment I, Agency Assessment. Log. Version 1.1, October 11, 2026. The dated log of changes, errors found, open issues, and ideas held for later.\n\n## 1. About this document\n\n1.1 This is one of five documents for this folder. `docs/systems/TIME.md` states what the folder is. `docs/systems/TIME_OPERATIONS.md` states how the system runs. This document states how both came to say what they say.\n\n1.2 No entry is removed. A closed item is marked closed and stays.\n\n1.3 A version of this document was written on August 29, 2026. That version was never filed. This file is a new document. It does not reconstruct the August 29 version.\n\n1.4 A change to `TIME.md`, to `TIME_OPERATIONS.md`, to the Curriculum, or to the Research is entered here with the date and the reason.\n\n## 2. Changes\n\n### August 28, 2026\n\n2.1 Version 3 of Folder 01 Time. Time was defined as building a schedule from conversation. It measured how much of a client's time was accounted for. It held a four stage arc and five completion conditions.\n\n### October 9, 2026\n\n2.2 Decisions D-010 to D-014 in `docs/DECISIONS.md`. The daily Plan became the core of Time. A Plan is reviewed by the commitments on it. Success is not the number of commitments. The agent uses the Plan as the reason for contact. The agent does not follow up on every commitment.\n\n### October 10, 2026\n\n2.3 `LANGUAGE_STANDARD.md` Version 4. The arc was set at three stages. The standard ruled that the agent is a person. The person is named a client in agent documents and a resident in text the person reads.\n\n2.4 Decisions D-015 to D-017. Entry runs Application, Enrollment, Orientation, then Time. Daily Close is placed on the Plan. The first Plan includes a check of how realistic it feels.\n\n2.5 The conditions of a commitment were set: Timing, Completion, Consequences, and Standards. Follow through was added as the fifth. A commitment, a follow up, and ongoing work were defined as three separate entries.\n\n### October 11, 2026\n\n2.6 Version 4 of Folder 01 Time. Time was redefined as planning, execution, review, and follow through. The schedule stayed as the system. Reason: the August 28 definition measured accounted hours, and the decisions of October 9 and 10 moved the folder to what happens after a Plan is made.\n\n2.7 Four departures from the revision plan, each required by `LANGUAGE_STANDARD.md`. The arc has three stages. The schedule is the system and Time is the folder. The daily check is named Daily Close, since Review names the meeting. The section once named Completion is named Operational conditions.\n\n2.8 Six corrections after the first review of Version 4.\n\n| Section | Correction |\n| :-- | :-- |\n| 3.3 | One state label was replaced by four separate outcome fields: Execution, Timing, Consequences, and Alternative |\n| 5.5 and 5.6 | A change to the client's file keeps the existing approval rules. Action permissions were assigned to Operations |\n| 5.9 | The Version 3 rules for order and frequency are held until the Curriculum is rewritten |\n| 7 | A day with no entry gets a question only where an open requirement depends on it |\n| 10 | The condition that required a follow up was removed |\n| 11.6 | Partial execution and an agreed alternative each stay on file as their own information |\n\n2.9 The fourteen items that the first draft of Version 4 listed as open. Twelve are closed. Item 15.1 stays open. Item 15.14 is closed by the creation of this file and of `TIME_OPERATIONS.md`.\n\n| First draft item | Subject | Resolution | Now in `TIME.md` |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- | :-- |\n| 15.1 | The twelve questions | Open | 15.1 |\n| 15.2 | The question for Time | Closed. Both questions are kept. One belongs to Daily Close and one to the schedule | 2.8 |\n| 15.3 | Time and Integrity | Closed. Integrity covers every commitment and reads Time. It holds no second copy | 4.4.7, 4.4.8, 13.10 |\n| 15.4 | A purchase amount | Closed. Filed in Finance | 4.4.6, 13.9 |\n| 15.5 | Partial execution, alternatives, timing | Closed. Execution is measured against the Completion condition. A changed time with no harm on file is successful. Corrected the same day. See 2.16 | 3.3.7, 11.6 |\n| 15.6 | Fields before a Plan | Closed. The action, the client's authorization, and a date or time | 3.3.4 |\n| 15.7 | What opens the Resident Portal home | Closed. The first seven day schedule and the first confirmed Plan | 9.3 |\n| 15.8 | Action with no further approval | Closed in principle. The full list of permissions is assigned to Operations | 5.6 |\n| 15.9 | When the agent stops asking | Closed in principle. No fixed count. The criteria are assigned to Operations | 7.16, 7.17 |\n| 15.10 | Morning contact | Closed. Three points of contact in the day. The client chooses how much | 5.4 |\n| 15.11 | The sender of a message | Closed. An automated message is shown as automated. The label is assigned to Operations | 5.4.12 |\n| 15.12 | Operational conditions | Closed. Three conditions. The two figures are produced by the schedule and are not conditions | 10 |\n| 15.13 | The free first day | Closed. Retired | 9.2 |\n| 15.14 | Operations and Developments | Closed. Both files were created in the repository on October 11, 2026 | 1.3 |\n\n2.10 Three further corrections. Consequences now tells none confirmed apart from not established. Authorization now separates preparation, authorized execution, and approval of a permanent change. Follow through that a standing permission covers runs with no question to the client.\n\n2.11 Three rulings by the founder on October 11, 2026.\n\n2.11.1 The repository holds the authoritative specification. `TIME.md` is the finished specification. Operations holds the mechanics. Developments holds how the thinking changed. A Word file is an export.\n\n2.11.2 Integrity covers every commitment the client made. It is not limited to work. Integrity reads the commitments on file in Time and creates no second schedule and no second copy.\n\n2.11.3 Software can carry out an action a standing permission covers, with no person releasing it. The rule that the agent is a person concerns identity and responsibility. A person is required in the four cases in `TIME.md` 5.6.6.\n\n2.12 Dates and vocabulary were set to United States English under `LANGUAGE_STANDARD.md` 5.9. Dates are written month, day, year.\n\n2.13 `TIME.md` was replaced. Until October 11, 2026 the file held a working product model. It now holds the specification. The parts of the working model that the specification does not carry are listed in section 5.\n\n2.14 `TIME_OPERATIONS.md` Version 1 and this document Version 1 were created.\n\n2.15 The four items left open in `TIME.md` section 15 were reviewed. The Curriculum stays open and is the next step. The Research stays open and follows the Curriculum. The block statuses moved to `TIME_OPERATIONS.md` 2.1.4. The Version 3 rule of lowest position first was retired, with the rule that set frequency by the twelve answers. `TIME.md` 5.9 now states four factors for the next question.\n\n2.16 Successful execution was split in two. Executed means the Completion condition is met. Confirmed successful means executed with Consequences none confirmed. Reason: the earlier wording counted a commitment with no information about consequences as successful. `TIME.md` 11.6.\n\n2.17 The sender rule in `TIME.md` 5.4.12 was reviewed and kept. A message from the agent and an automated Universe City message stay distinct.\n\n2.18 `LANGUAGE_STANDARD.md` 3.20 was changed to match `TIME.md` 5.9. Position now informs the assessment and identifies where work exists. Clauses 3.20.1 to 3.20.3 were added. Clauses 3.12 and 12.7 and the change record of the standard carry the date. Start, Target, Position, Report, and Grade keep their definitions. `TIME.md` was not changed. Ruled by the founder.\n\n2.19 **Calendar-first presentation of Time, founder clarification, October 11, 2026.** The founder observed in Founder Test Session 01 that Time's facts were being filed without a resident-facing working calendar. The founder reaffirmed that the first Time product is a calendar and schedule, populated as the guide learns existing obligations and establishes commitments. `TIME.md` already identified the seven-day schedule as the system in 11.1–11.4. The clarification adds required day/week/month visual views and progression in `TIME.md` 2.9.1–2.9.2, 9.3.1, and 11.4.1–11.4.2. It does not replace the planning, execution, Daily Close, Review, or follow-through work that runs on the schedule.\n\n2.20 **Operations Version 1.1 and Time Version 4.1, October 11, 2026.** `TIME_OPERATIONS.md` now separates the existing numerical week summary from the unverified visual weekly calendar, specifies the day/week/month product requirements, the shared canonical Time record, access and editing constraints, unknown-time display, and acceptance tests. The exact unresolved mechanics remain explicitly To define, and the work in Claude's founder-test branch remains unverified. Reason: prevent a page of stored facts or a week-totals table from being described as a finished Time system.\n\n2.21 **Existing Curriculum and Research located in Google Drive, October 11, 2026.** The Google document titled `Folder 01 Time Curriculum (4)` contains **Curriculum Version 2**, dated October 11, 2026, with twelve revised questions and a Schedule intake in section 18. It says that Time intake begins after Orientation and assembles the first seven-day schedule through conversation. `Folder 01 Time Research (3)` contains updated dated entries, including October 11 research verification. Both remain outside the GitHub repository. This corrects the outdated repository status claim that neither was revised and both still covered Version 3. Their content and source evidence have not been replaced by this revision; their official integration and approval status remain to resolve.\n\n2.22 **Curriculum and Research added to the repository, October 11, 2026.** On the founder's instruction, the Drive copies `Folder 01 Time Curriculum (4)` (Curriculum Version 2) and `Folder 01 Time Research (3)` were copied once, word for word, into `docs/systems/TIME_CURRICULUM.md` and `docs/systems/TIME_RESEARCH.md`. GitHub now holds all five documents for this folder, and the repository copy governs. Drive is no longer read for them. The founder test guide reads the five documents through the `read_time_document` action, from a copy made by `scripts/pilot-docs.mjs`; a test fails when that copy and the files differ. The content of the Curriculum and the Research was not changed. Their own open items stay open, including Curriculum 22.10 on the repository, which this entry answers in part.\n\n## 3. Errors found\n\n3.1 October 11, 2026. Version 3 listed five features as live that the repository does not hold: plain language collection, the day view by whole hours, the month view, direct editing by hand, and reminders. Corrected in `TIME.md` 14.3.\n\n3.2 October 11, 2026. Version 3 item 15.2 asked for a field to be renamed. The code already names it `cost_of`. Closed.\n\n3.3 October 11, 2026. The first draft of Version 4 held one label for execution, timing, and consequences. Corrected. See 2.8.\n\n3.4 October 11, 2026. The first draft of Version 4 made a follow up a condition of the folder being operational. A client whose commitments produce no further work could never have met it. Corrected. See 2.8.\n\n3.5 October 11, 2026. A comment in `lib/domain/time.ts` states twelve kinds of time. The code holds eleven. Open.\n\n3.6 October 11, 2026. `TIME.md` 11.6 counted an executed commitment as successful while its consequences were not established. Corrected. See 2.16.\n\n3.7 October 11, 2026. The existing week view was a numerical table of time figures, not a visual seven-day grid. The day and month calendar and direct editing were also not built according to the prior repository inventory. The founder-test File listed commitments but lacked the corresponding working calendar. The requirement was stated in the system definition but not fully carried into the UI implementation contract. Product and Operations were clarified; actual preview behavior remains to verify.\n\n3.8 October 11, 2026. An earlier status statement that the Curriculum and Research had not been revised was out of date when this documentation review found the October 11 Google Drive copies. Previous entries are preserved for history; see 2.21 and 4.8.\n\n## 4. Open issues\n\n4.1 `TIME.md` section 15 holds four open items: the Curriculum, the Research, the block statuses, and the Version 3 rules for order and frequency. Closed in part on October 11, 2026. See 2.15. Two items remain open: the Curriculum and the Research.\n\n4.2 `TIME_OPERATIONS.md` marks each undecided mechanic as To define.\n\n4.3 The Curriculum and the Research are not in the repository. Both still describe Version 3. Neither has been revised. Superseded October 11, 2026: see 2.21 and 2.22. Both are in `docs/systems`.\n\n4.4 Decisions D-010 to D-017 call the person a resident. `TIME.md` calls the person a client, as `LANGUAGE_STANDARD.md` requires for an agent document. The two words name one person.\n\n4.5 `LANGUAGE_STANDARD.md` 3.20 states that order of work is determined by position. `TIME.md` 5.9 retires that rule for Time. `LANGUAGE_STANDARD.md` 12.7 lists clause 3.20 as kept from Version 3 without review. The standard governs until its clause is changed. Closed October 11, 2026. See 2.18.\n\n4.6 `LANGUAGE_STANDARD.md` 3.20 keeps a third output, engagement level, which sets how much the agent asks for. `TIME.md` 5.9.3 retires the Version 3 rule that set how often the agent asks by the twelve answers. The two overlap. The standard lists the engagement level output as kept without review. Open.\n\n4.7 The Curriculum of August 28, 2026 holds a table for each question that states what the agent does at each start band. Those tables rest on the retired rules. They are part of the Curriculum revision in `TIME.md` 15.1. Open in this prior review; see updated status in 4.8.\n\n4.8 **October 11, 2026, status correction.** A Google Drive Curriculum Version 2 and updated Research are now identified. The earlier conclusion in 4.3 that both were unrevised Version 3 is superseded. Curriculum Version 2 section 18 already addresses building the first seven-day schedule and section 20.7 says no lesson is a condition for the schedule or a Plan. Remaining work is to reconcile the new *visual calendar* experience with the existing Schedule intake and teaching text, review approval status, and bring authoritative source copies into the repository under the five-document convention. Do not rewrite the twelve questions or the Research evidence merely to describe the UI.\n\n4.9 **Calendar code review open.** The founder-test calendar is under development; verify all three views, their time positioning, an unknown end time, manual edits, the one-record contract, permission and revision preservation, and full Time intake progression before declaring the system built. The running prototype must also preserve a pending or tentative commitment without prematurely changing a schedule entry. See Founder Test issue #29 and `TIME_OPERATIONS.md` 4.5–4.15.\n\n4.10 **Interface-shell decision separate.** The founder also requested full-center Conversation and a right-side decisions/context panel. This updates the direction of the founder test and conflicts with the earlier `docs/product/RESIDENT_INTERFACE.md` right-side Agent default. Record that discrepancy for a later deliberate interface-document ruling after preview; it is not a change to Time's record semantics. See Founder Test issue #30.\n\n## 5. Ideas held for later\n\n5.1 Each idea below was in the working product model that `TIME.md` held until October 11, 2026. None is in the specification. None is decided.\n\n5.2 A question about what makes a commitment hard, asked for a commitment the client wants to change. Then a question about what to do if that happens.\n\n5.3 Four planning categories for the agent's own use: fixed, maintain, move, and protect. The working model did not approve them for text a resident reads.\n\n5.4 Suggestions built from the client's own outcomes. The working model required five parts in a suggestion: a priority the client stated, evidence from the client's own outcomes, open time that exists, one specific commitment, and the client's choice.\n\n5.5 A statement of fact when a client does what they said they would do. `LANGUAGE_STANDARD.md` 10.6.2 prohibits praise for a result. The wording needs a ruling before it is used.\n\n5.6 A one line summary of the whole day at Daily Close.\n\n5.7 A record of the patterns in a client's outcomes, kept for planning. The folder Instinct in Life Management may hold the same record.\n\nGoverned by `LANGUAGE_STANDARD.md`, section 8.1.6, for composition.\n"},{"key":"curriculum","title":"Folder 01 Time Curriculum","path":"docs/systems/TIME_CURRICULUM.md","text":"**Folder 01: Time, Curriculum**\n\n*Department I, Agency Assessment. Internal. Version 2, October 11, 2026. The twelve questions, their options, the Schedule intake, and the teaching layer.*\n\n# 1. About this document\n**1.1**  This is one of five documents for this folder. It holds what the client is asked and what the client is taught: the twelve questions, their options, the Schedule intake, and the teaching layer. It is read by the agent and by the person who builds the folder.\n\n**1.2**  The other four. **Folder 01 Time** states what the folder is. **Folder 01 Time Operations** holds the fields, the statuses, the permissions, and the contact settings. **Folder 01 Time Research** holds the evidence. **Folder 01 Time Developments** holds the dated log of changes.\n\n**1.3**  This document does not restate the other four. Where a rule is held in one of them, this document names the clause.\n\n**1.4**  Where this document and Folder 01 Time disagree, Folder 01 Time governs. The Language Standard governs the wording of both.\n\n**1.5**  The questions, the options, the lines beneath them, the definitions, and what each lesson says are in the official layer of the Language Standard. They are never reworded for a client. Language Standard 4.3 and 4.13.4.\n\n**1.6**  Guided Life is an optional way to carry out a Plan with the guide. It is no subject of assessment and it adds no question. Folder 01 Time, 5.11.\n\n**1.7**  Version 2 is dated October 11, 2026. It replaces Version 1 of August 28, 2026. Version 1 asked how well each of twelve kinds of time was accounted for. Version 2 asks how a client plans a commitment, carries it out, reviews what happened, and follows through on the work that remains.\n\n**1.8**  The twelve subjects of Version 1 are kept. They moved to the Schedule intake in section 18. They are no longer the twelve questions.\n\n**1.9**  The twelve questions have not been tested with clients. Folder 01 Time Research states that related research does not validate them. Section 22 lists what a test would settle.\n\n**1.10**  Client copy is text the client reads. In this document it is the question, each Start option, the line beneath it, each Target option, and the definitions and the instruction in 5.4. It appears in the exact words the client sees.\n\n# 2. What the scale measures\n**2.1**  The scale measures how a client plans a commitment, carries it out, reviews what happened, and follows through on the work that remains. Each one is measured under the conditions the client set. Folder 01 Time, 2.3 and 2.6.\n\n**2.2**  Four measures are never used.\n\n**2.2.1  The number of commitments.** A Plan with two commitments and a Plan with ten can sit at the same option. Rest counts the same as every other commitment. Folder 01 Time, 2.7.\n\n**2.2.2  Exact punctuality.** A start or a finish is compared with the timing the client set. No punctuality rule applies to every client.\n\n**2.2.3  Doing it with no support.** A reminder, the schedule, a list, a calendar, the agent, the guide, a person who helps the client, and work done under a standing permission count at every option.\n\n**2.2.4  How much the client writes down.** The agent keeps the file. No option asks the client to keep a log.\n\n**2.3**  Each scale measures one ability. Each question states its progression: the one measure that rises, and the ten levels in order, one for each option. A higher option is a higher level of that ability. It is never a second ability.\n\n**2.4**  Option 1 states that the subject is not functioning. Option 10 states that the subject worked through the past month under the client’s conditions, and what happened to an exception. Option 10 does not require a month with no exception. Language Standard 9.6.\n\n**2.5**  No option requires a condition that an employer, an institution, or a second person sets for the client. Each option is written so that it is true inside those conditions. A client can reach every option, and can select option 10 as a Target, inside conditions that other people set. Language Standard 9.8.2.\n\n**2.6**  An option is true when it is true with the support the client uses. A client who looks at the schedule, asks the agent, uses Guided Life, or has work done under a standing permission meets the option. The client’s own part is to report, to decide, to give a permission, and to do the action only the client can do. Folder 01 Time, 5.5.6.\n\n**2.7**  Agent assistance is never a lower level. No option ranks doing a task alone above doing it with the agent, with the guide, with a reminder, or with work done under a standing permission. Language Standard 9.8.1.\n\n**2.8**  About half and most describe a share of the client’s own planned commitments. They never describe a count.\n\n**2.9**  Start and Target describe the same level at each number. Target 4 is Start 4 stated as a condition the client chooses. Target 1 is stated as dealing with the subject when it comes up. Language Standard 9.11.\n\n**2.10**  The client selects one option for Start and one option for Target. Start is the option that is true of the client today. Target is the option the client intends to reach. Language Standard 3.14.\n\n**2.11**  The Target is the client’s decision. A client who selects a Target of 5 has stated how far they want this subject to go. The agent works to that Target.\n\n**2.12**  A low Start describes the subject today. The agent does not treat a low Start as a judgment of the client. Language Standard 6.14.\n\n## 2.13 The Enrollment report and the outcomes on file\n**2.13.1**  The client’s ability is assessed twice. Folder 01 Time, 2.5. The two assessments are kept apart.\n\n**2.13.2  At Enrollment.** The Start and the Target are the client’s own report. The client selects from what they know of themselves. No outcome on file is required before the client selects an option. No option is held back.\n\n**2.13.3  About half and most.** At Enrollment these words are the client’s own estimate of a share. The client is not asked to prove the share. The founder ruled this on October 11, 2026.\n\n**2.13.4  Once Time is operating.** The agent assesses what is happening from the outcomes on file. The agent does not use the Enrollment Start as evidence of what happened.\n\n**2.13.5  The Enrollment Start is kept.** The agent and software never replace it, correct it, or overwrite it. It stays on file with its date. Language Standard 3.16.\n\n**2.13.6**  Where the outcomes on file and the Enrollment Start disagree, both stay on file. The agent works from the outcomes. The agent states the outcomes and does not tell the client that the Start was wrong. Language Standard 6.3.\n\n**2.13.7**  Only the client selects a Start. Section 22 holds the question of whether a client selects a second Start later.\n\n**2.13.8**  Folder 01 Time Research found that two facts cannot be measured before outcomes exist: how close an estimate is, and how often a Plan is carried out. A client still selects those options at Enrollment, as a self report.\n\n# 3. How the answers direct the agent\n**3.1**  The answers produce three outputs. Language Standard 3.20.\n\n**3.1.1  Position.** Start minus Target. Position shows where work exists.\n\n**3.1.2  Baseline.** What the client already does and what is already on file. The agent does not request what is already on file.\n\n**3.1.3  Engagement level.** How much the agent asks the client for on one subject.\n\n**3.2**  Two rules of Version 1 are retired. Folder 01 Time, 5.9.\n\n**3.2.1  Lowest position first.** Position does not set the order of work. The agent determines the next action from five factors: the information still missing, the commitments the client has established, the importance of the work to the client, the consequences on file, and the client’s preferences. Language Standard 3.20.2.\n\n**3.2.2  A low Start means more frequent questions.** The column How often it asks is removed from each table. How often the client is contacted follows the client’s contact preferences. Folder 01 Time, 5.4.\n\n**3.3**  Engagement level and contact frequency are two separate settings. Engagement level comes from the twelve answers. Contact frequency comes from the client. A low Start never raises how often the client is contacted.\n\n**3.4**  A need that is urgent or tied to a date is addressed when it arises, whatever its position. Language Standard 3.20.3.\n\n**3.5**  Each question carries a table. The table states where the agent begins at each Start. Once outcomes are on file, the agent works from the outcomes.\n\n**3.6**  Each row is carried out inside three limits: the client’s decisions, the client’s contact preferences, and the standing permissions on file. Where a row and one of the three disagree, the limit governs. Folder 01 Time, 5.4.9, 5.6, and 8.7.\n\n**3.7**  The agent does the same administrative work at every Start. The table changes what the agent asks about. It never reduces the support a client receives, and it never treats support as a lower level.\n\n**3.8**  The agent’s administrative work stays with the agent. The agent does the administrative work the client has authorized, schedules the work that remains, and keeps each open follow up active. Folder 01 Time, 2.4.3.\n\n**3.9**  The client is asked for four kinds of input only: missing information, a decision that belongs to the client, a permission, and an action only the client can do. Folder 01 Time, 5.5.6.\n\n**3.10**  No question, no option, and no lesson asks the client to keep a second copy of what the agent keeps.\n\n**3.11**  Work that the agent or software carried out under a standing permission counts toward the client’s options. A client is never placed at a lower option because the agent did the administrative part.\n\n# 4. How the agent talks with the client\n**4.1**  The client answers the twelve questions at Enrollment. Folder 01 Time, 9.2. After Enrollment the agent learns the client’s circumstances through ordinary conversation. Folder 01 Time, 5.1.\n\n**4.2**  The agent asks one question at a time. The agent does not ask for information already on file. Folder 01 Time, 6.2.\n\n**4.3**  Every question has a purpose before it is asked. The purpose is one missing piece. Folder 01 Time, 6.1.\n\n**4.4**  A message is short and holds one thought until the client asks for a different length. The client can ask for more conversation, more explanation, a different pace, or silence. Language Standard 4.5.\n\n**4.5**  The agent acknowledges what the client said before the agent raises a pattern or an analysis. Acknowledgment and natural encouragement are permitted. The agent invents no praise, judges no client, and claims no outcome that has not occurred. Language Standard 10.6.2.\n\n**4.6**  A pattern that is not urgent waits until the client has finished the present subject.\n\n**4.7**  A pattern is stated only from outcomes on file. The agent states the count and the dates. The agent does not state a cause. Language Standard 6.3.\n\n**4.8**  An urgent matter is raised at once. A matter is urgent when it is tied to a date that is near or to a consequence on file.\n\n**4.9**  Where a fact is on file, the agent states it. Where a fact is uncertain, the agent asks.\n\n**4.10**  The five conditions of a commitment are internal requirements. They are never asked as five questions. Folder 01 Time, 3.3.3.\n\n**4.11**  The agent asks about a commitment by name. The client is never asked to reconstruct a whole day from memory. Folder 01 Time, 5.4.7. The Version 1 instructions to reconstruct a night or an evening hour by hour are retired.\n\n**4.12**  The agent does not comment on how the client spends time. The agent does not describe a position, a grade, or a figure from the assessment as good or bad. Folder 01 Time, 8.2. Language Standard 6.14.3.\n\n**4.13**  The client decides what goes on the Plan, what each commitment requires, and what changes. Folder 01 Time, 8.7.\n\n**4.14**  The agent can recommend a practical next action, and can recommend before the client asks. A recommendation is not a decision. The client accepts it, changes it, or declines it. Folder 01 Time, 5.10. Language Standard 10.11.\n\n**4.15**  When the client asks for help or says they cannot start, the agent recommends one step and puts the whole Plan aside. The Plan returns when the client asks for it. Folder 01 Time, 5.13.4.\n\n**4.16**  The agent and the guide adapt wording, warmth, pace, humor, directness, the depth of conversation, and the style of guidance to the client and to the activity. No adaptation changes a fact on file, a permission, the client’s authority, an option, or a score. Language Standard 4.9 to 4.11.\n\n**4.17**  The guide is software. It is identified as software and it is separate from the agent. Each rule in this section applies to the guide. Folder 01 Time, 5.2.\n\n**4.18**  The standard wording of the agent’s questions is held in Folder 01 Time, section 6. This document adds no question to it. The agent and the guide can adapt that wording. The rules in Folder 01 Time, 6.14, hold in every wording.\n\n## 4.19 One case\n**4.19.1**  This is one case. It shows one acknowledgment, one question, and one offer. The open block is on the client’s file.\n\nClient: The grocery shopping did not happen today.\n\nAgent: Okay.\n\nAgent: Is the grocery shopping still needed?\n\nClient: Yes.\n\nAgent: You have an open block tomorrow at 10:00 AM.\n\nAgent: Do you want me to move the grocery shopping to tomorrow at 10:00 AM?\n\n# 5. The twelve questions\n**5.1**  The twelve follow the definition of the folder. Questions one to four cover planning. Questions five to seven cover carrying out a commitment. Questions eight and nine cover review. Questions ten and eleven cover follow through. Question twelve covers the support the client uses in all four.\n\n| No. | Question | What it measures |\n| :---- | :---- | :---- |\n| 1 | Commitments | Commitments known ahead |\n| 2 | Taking on a commitment | Taking on new commitments |\n| 3 | The plan | A plan made ahead |\n| 4 | Time a commitment takes | A commitment’s full time |\n| 5 | Starting | Starting what was planned |\n| 6 | Completing | Finishing what was planned |\n| 7 | Changes | Handling a changed plan |\n| 8 | Review | Knowing what happened |\n| 9 | Consequences | What a change affects |\n| 10 | Remaining work | Work left after |\n| 11 | Recurring responsibilities | Recurring responsibilities |\n| 12 | Using support | Use of support |\n\n**5.2**  Each question measures one subject. The subject is stated in four words or fewer. Language Standard 9.3.\n\n**5.3**  Twelve abilities were proposed on October 11, 2026. Three changes were made to them. Estimates and Preparation and travel became one question, Time a commitment takes. Available time became Taking on a commitment. Using support was added. Folder 01 Time Research, entry of October 11, 2026, section 5, gives the reason for each change.\n\n## 5.4 Client copy, six terms and one instruction\n**5.4.1**  The client reads these six definitions and this instruction with the questions.\n\nCommitment. A specific action you intend to carry out.\n\nPlan. The commitments you intend to carry out on one day.\n\nSet. A commitment is set when it has a day or a time.\n\nTake on. You take on a commitment when you agree to it or when it is given to you.\n\nRecurring responsibility. Work that comes due again after it is done.\n\nSupport. A reminder, your schedule, a list, a calendar, your agent, Guided Life, or a person who helps you. Work that is done for you with your permission is support.\n\nAnswer for what happens with the support you use now. An option is true when it is true with that support.\n\n**5.4.2**  Commitment and Plan follow Folder 01 Time, 3.3.1 and 5.3.1. Carry out stands for execute. Folder 01 Time, 3.3.1, defines execution as the carrying out of a commitment.\n\n**5.4.3**  Recurring responsibility is the term in Folder 01 Time, 7.6. Client copy and this document use that one term.\n\n**5.4.4**  Support names the schedule, the agent, Guided Life, and work done under a standing permission, so that the definition matches section 2. Guided Life is defined in the Language Standard, section 2.\n\n**5.4.5**  Set keeps a commitment the client has not been told about out of the scale. Take on covers a commitment the client is given.\n\n**5.4.6**  The Language Standard, section 2, defines none of the six. Section 22 holds the question.\n\n**5.4.7**  Rest is a commitment when the client plans it. Folder 01 Time, 2.7.\n\n# 6. Question one: Commitments\n**6.1  Measures:** commitments known ahead.\n\n**6.2  Asked as:** How far ahead do you know your commitments?\n\n**6.3  Progression:** How far ahead the client knows a commitment that is already set, and then how current that knowledge is kept. The ten levels in order: 1 learned after its time has passed; 2 learned on the day it is due; 3 today known at the start of the day; 4 tomorrow known; 5 three days known; 6 seven days known; 7 seven days known with times and places; 8 known past seven days; 9 a new or changed commitment added the day it is set; 10 known ahead for a month, with a late arrival added when it arrived.\n\n## 6.4 Start options\nThe client selects the one that is true of them today.\n\n**1.  I find out about a commitment after its time has passed.**\n\nYou learn of a commitment once it is over.\n\n**2.  I find out about a commitment on the day it is due.**\n\nYou learn of it that day.\n\n**3.  At the start of my day, I can say what is set for today.**\n\nToday is known when it starts.\n\n**4.  I can say what is set for tomorrow.**\n\nOne day ahead is known.\n\n**5.  I can say what is set for the next three days.**\n\nThree days ahead are known.\n\n**6.  I can say what is set for the next seven days.**\n\nA week ahead is known.\n\n**7.  I can say the time and the place of what is set for the next seven days.**\n\nThe week is known with its times and places.\n\n**8.  I can say what is set past the next seven days.**\n\nCommitments further out are known.\n\n**9.  A new or changed commitment is added to the place I keep my commitments on the day it is set.**\n\nWhat you know stays current.\n\n**10.  For the past month I knew my commitments ahead of time. One that arrived late was added when it arrived.**\n\nKnowing your commitments has worked for a month.\n\n## 6.5 Target options\nEach completes the phrase my target is to. The client selects the one they are aiming for.\n\n**1.**  Deal with a commitment when it comes up.\n\n**2.**  Find out about a commitment on the day it is due.\n\n**3.**  Be able to say at the start of my day what is set for today.\n\n**4.**  Be able to say what is set for tomorrow.\n\n**5.**  Be able to say what is set for the next three days.\n\n**6.**  Be able to say what is set for the next seven days.\n\n**7.**  Be able to say the time and the place of what is set for the next seven days.\n\n**8.**  Be able to say what is set past the next seven days.\n\n**9.**  Have a new or changed commitment added on the day it is set.\n\n**10.**  Know my commitments ahead of time for a month, and have one that arrives late added when it arrives.\n\n## 6.6 What the agent does\n| Start | Where the agent begins |\n| :---- | :---- |\n| 1 to 2 | Builds the list with the client from what the client says and what is on file. Does not ask the client to write it. Offers a reminder for the next commitment. |\n| 3 to 4 | Adds the commitments the client reports for the days after tomorrow. Presents tomorrow’s commitments at the contact the client chose. |\n| 5 to 6 | Asks for a time or a place only where one is missing. |\n| 7 to 8 | Files a commitment past the week when the client reports it. Checks that a new or changed commitment reached the schedule. |\n| 9 to 10 | Does not collect. Acts when a commitment arrives with no time, or overlaps a block. |\n\n## 6.7 Teaching\n**6.7.1**  Lesson 19.23 applies. It is taught when a commitment was missed and no reminder was set for it.\n\n## 6.8 Notes\n**6.8.1**  The agent builds the list and keeps it. A client who looks at the schedule or asks the agent can say what is set. That counts at every option.\n\n**6.8.2**  A commitment the client has not been told about is not set. A client who learns a shift two days ahead can select option 6 when the rest of the week is known.\n\n**6.8.3**  Option 10 allows a commitment that arrives late. It asks what happened to that commitment.\n\n**6.8.4**  What a commitment is about is filed in the folder for that subject. Folder 01 Time, section 4, states where.\n\n# 7. Question two: Taking on a commitment\n**7.1  Measures:** taking on new commitments.\n\n**7.2  Asked as:** When you take on a new commitment, what do you find out about your time?\n\n**7.3  Progression:** How much of the client’s time is checked when a new commitment is taken on, and then whether a small commitment gets the same check. The ten levels in order: 1 no check; 2 a check afterward; 3 the hour; 4 the day; 5 the week; 6 the full time of the commitment; 7 what it moves or replaces; 8 what changes decided the same day; 9 the same check for a small commitment; 10 the effect known and the change decided for a month.\n\n## 7.4 Start options\nThe client selects the one that is true of them today.\n\n**1.  I take on a new commitment and do not look at my time.**\n\nNo check happens.\n\n**2.  I look at my time after I have taken on a new commitment.**\n\nThe check comes afterward.\n\n**3.  When I take on a new commitment, I find out whether that hour is already taken.**\n\nThe hour is checked.\n\n**4.  When I take on a new commitment, I find out what else is set for that day.**\n\nThe day is checked.\n\n**5.  When I take on a new commitment, I find out what else is set for that week.**\n\nThe week is checked.\n\n**6.  When I take on a new commitment, I find out its full time: getting ready, travel, and the time I need after it.**\n\nThe time around it is known.\n\n**7.  When I take on a new commitment, I can say what it moves or replaces.**\n\nYou know what it takes time from.\n\n**8.  When a new commitment does not fit, I decide what changes on the day I take it on.**\n\nThe decision is made that day.\n\n**9.  My small commitments get the same check as my large ones.**\n\nA small commitment gets the same check.\n\n**10.  For the past month I knew what a new commitment took from my time when I took it on. I decided what changed when one did not fit.**\n\nTaking on commitments has worked for a month.\n\n## 7.5 Target options\nEach completes the phrase my target is to. The client selects the one they are aiming for.\n\n**1.**  Deal with a new commitment when its day comes.\n\n**2.**  Look at my time after taking on a new commitment.\n\n**3.**  Find out whether the hour is already taken when taking on a new commitment.\n\n**4.**  Find out what else is set for that day when taking on a new commitment.\n\n**5.**  Find out what else is set for that week when taking on a new commitment.\n\n**6.**  Find out the full time of a new commitment when taking it on.\n\n**7.**  Be able to say what a new commitment moves or replaces.\n\n**8.**  Decide what changes on the day a commitment that does not fit is taken on.\n\n**9.**  Have my small commitments get the same check as my large ones.\n\n**10.**  Know for a month what a new commitment takes from my time, and decide what changes when one does not fit.\n\n## 7.6 What the agent does\n| Start | Where the agent begins |\n| :---- | :---- |\n| 1 to 2 | When the client reports a new commitment, states what is already on the schedule in that time. Can recommend where the commitment fits. |\n| 3 to 4 | States what else is set for that day and that week. |\n| 5 to 6 | Adds the time to get ready, the travel, and the time after, as costs of the new commitment, where the client states them or a standing permission covers it. |\n| 7 to 8 | States what the new commitment overlaps. Asks which one holds. Folder 01 Time, 7.7. |\n| 9 to 10 | Does not prompt. Acts on an overlap, or on a commitment that takes the controlled hours figure below the client’s target. Folder 01 Time, 7.13. |\n\n## 7.7 Teaching\n**7.7.1**  Lessons 19.10, 19.11, and 19.13 apply. Each is taught when the client is deciding on a new commitment and a week of the schedule is on file.\n\n**7.7.2**  Lesson 19.12 applies when the new commitment needs a long block.\n\n## 7.8 Notes\n**7.8.1**  Take on covers a commitment the client agrees to and a commitment the client is given. No option assumes the client can refuse a commitment.\n\n**7.8.2**  A client who tells the agent about a new commitment and hears what it overlaps has found out. That counts at options 3 to 7.\n\n**7.8.3**  The agent states what is on the schedule and can recommend where a commitment fits. The decision to take a commitment is the client’s. Saying no is not a level of this scale.\n\n**7.8.4**  No option asks the client to keep time open. Open time depends on conditions the client may not control.\n\n**7.8.5**  A client’s rule for which decisions wait is filed in Life Management, Discernment. This question measures the check against time.\n\n# 8. Question three: The plan\n**8.1  Measures:** a plan made ahead.\n\n**8.2  Asked as:** Before a day starts, how much of it have you planned?\n\n**8.3  Progression:** How complete the plan is before the day starts. The ten levels in order: 1 no plan; 2 a plan made once a commitment is at risk; 3 the commitments with a set time; 4 the open time; 5 an order; 6 times; 7 a check against the hours in the day; 8 the place and what is needed; 9 a second option; 10 a plan ahead for a month on the days chosen for planning, changed when it did not fit.\n\n## 8.4 Start options\nThe client selects the one that is true of them today.\n\n**1.  I start a day with no plan for it.**\n\nThe day is decided as it happens.\n\n**2.  I make a plan once a commitment is already at risk.**\n\nPlanning starts when a problem is near.\n\n**3.  Before a day starts, I have a plan that names the commitments with a set time.**\n\nThe fixed commitments are on the plan.\n\n**4.  Before a day starts, my plan also names what I intend to do in the open time.**\n\nThe rest of the day is on the plan.\n\n**5.  Before a day starts, my plan is in an order.**\n\nThe day has an order.\n\n**6.  Before a day starts, my plan has a time for what is on it.**\n\nThe plan has times.\n\n**7.  Before a day starts, my plan has been checked against the hours in the day.**\n\nThe plan is checked against the time you have.\n\n**8.  Before a day starts, my plan says where I need to be and what I need to have with me.**\n\nThe place and the supplies are on the plan.\n\n**9.  Before a day starts, my plan says what I will do if a commitment cannot happen.**\n\nA second option is decided ahead.\n\n**10.  For the past month I had a plan before the day started, on the days I chose to plan. I changed a plan that did not fit the day.**\n\nPlanning has worked for a month.\n\n## 8.5 Target options\nEach completes the phrase my target is to. The client selects the one they are aiming for.\n\n**1.**  Take a day as it comes.\n\n**2.**  Make a plan when a commitment is at risk.\n\n**3.**  Have a plan before a day starts that names the commitments with a set time.\n\n**4.**  Have a plan before a day starts that also names what to do in the open time.\n\n**5.**  Have a plan before a day starts that is in an order.\n\n**6.**  Have a plan before a day starts with a time for what is on it.\n\n**7.**  Have a plan before a day starts that has been checked against the hours in the day.\n\n**8.**  Have a plan before a day starts that says where to be and what to have with me.\n\n**9.**  Have a plan before a day starts that says what to do if a commitment cannot happen.\n\n**10.**  Plan ahead for a month on the days chosen for planning, and change a plan that does not fit the day.\n\n## 8.6 What the agent does\n| Start | Where the agent begins |\n| :---- | :---- |\n| 1 to 2 | Presents the commitments already on file for tomorrow. Asks what is different from usual. The client confirms the Plan. Folder 01 Time, 5.3.1. |\n| 3 to 4 | Asks what the client intends for the open time. Adds only what the client states. |\n| 5 to 6 | Proposes an order and times from the open blocks. The client confirms or changes them. |\n| 7 to 8 | Asks how realistic the Plan feels before it is saved. Adds the place and the supplies where the client states them. |\n| 9 to 10 | Confirms the Plan. Asks about a second option only for a commitment with a stated consequence. |\n\n## 8.7 Teaching\n**8.7.1**  Lesson 19.16 applies. It is taught before a Plan is saved, when the commitments on it take more time than the open hours of that day.\n\n**8.7.2**  Lesson 19.17 applies. It is taught when the client names a commitment they want to carry out and it has no time or no place.\n\n## 8.8 Notes\n**8.8.1**  A Plan the agent prepares and the client confirms is the client’s plan. It counts at every option.\n\n**8.8.2**  A Plan with two commitments is at the same option as a Plan with ten. Rest counts the same as every other commitment.\n\n**8.8.3**  No option assumes the client sets their own hours. A plan for a day that an employer or an institution fixed is a plan.\n\n**8.8.4**  A day with no open time meets option 4 when the plan holds the commitments with a set time.\n\n**8.8.5**  A day the client chooses to leave unplanned is the client’s choice. Option 10 describes the days the client chose to plan.\n\n# 9. Question four: Time a commitment takes\n**9.1  Measures:** a commitment’s full time.\n\n**9.2  Asked as:** How well do you know how long a commitment takes you in full?\n\n**9.3  Progression:** How much of the full time of a commitment the client knows, and then whether the figure comes from what happened and is kept current. The ten levels in order: 1 no figure; 2 the set length; 3 the time before; 4 the time after; 5 the full time of one commitment from a day it happened; 6 the full time of the commitments that come back; 7 the commitments whose time differs from the time allowed; 8 the plan uses the time taken before; 9 a different time reported or corrected the same day; 10 planned time close to the time taken for a month, with a wrong figure corrected.\n\n## 9.4 Start options\nThe client selects the one that is true of them today.\n\n**1.  I cannot say how long my commitments take.**\n\nNo figure exists.\n\n**2.  I can say how long a commitment is set to last.**\n\nThe set length is known.\n\n**3.  I can say the time I need before a commitment: getting ready and getting there.**\n\nThe time before is known.\n\n**4.  I can say the time I need after a commitment: getting back and the time before I can start the next one.**\n\nThe time after is known.\n\n**5.  I can say the full time one commitment took on a day it happened.**\n\nOne figure comes from what happened.\n\n**6.  I can say the full time the commitments that come back took on days they happened.**\n\nThe ones that come back have figures from what happened.\n\n**7.  I can say which commitments take a different amount of time than I allow.**\n\nYou know which figures are wrong.\n\n**8.  My plan uses the time a commitment took before.**\n\nThe plan uses your own figures.\n\n**9.  When a commitment takes a different amount of time than planned, I report it or correct it that day.**\n\nThe figures stay current.\n\n**10.  For the past month the time on my plan was close to the time my commitments took. A figure that was wrong was corrected.**\n\nYour figures have worked for a month.\n\n## 9.5 Target options\nEach completes the phrase my target is to. The client selects the one they are aiming for.\n\n**1.**  Deal with how long a commitment takes when it happens.\n\n**2.**  Be able to say how long a commitment is set to last.\n\n**3.**  Be able to say the time needed before a commitment: getting ready and getting there.\n\n**4.**  Be able to say the time needed after a commitment: getting back and the time before the next one can start.\n\n**5.**  Be able to say the full time one commitment took on a day it happened.\n\n**6.**  Be able to say the full time the commitments that come back took on days they happened.\n\n**7.**  Be able to say which commitments take a different amount of time than allowed.\n\n**8.**  Have a plan that uses the time a commitment took before.\n\n**9.**  Report or correct the figure on the day a commitment takes a different amount of time than planned.\n\n**10.**  Have the time on my plan close to the time my commitments take for a month, with a wrong figure corrected.\n\n## 9.6 What the agent does\n| Start | Where the agent begins |\n| :---- | :---- |\n| 1 to 2 | Asks how long one recent commitment took in full. Files it as observed only when the client states what happened. |\n| 3 to 4 | Attaches the time before and the time after to a commitment, as costs of it. A trip is counted both ways. |\n| 5 to 6 | Shows the client’s estimate beside the observed time for the same commitment. States no direction. |\n| 7 to 8 | Uses the observed time in the next Plan where the client agrees or a standing permission covers it. |\n| 9 to 10 | Does not collect. Corrects a figure when the client reports a different time. |\n\n## 9.7 Teaching\n**9.7.1**  Lesson 19.14 applies. It is taught when a commitment with travel is on file and the client is planning the same commitment again.\n\n**9.7.2**  Lesson 19.15 applies. It is taught when an estimate and an observed time for one commitment are both on file.\n\n## 9.8 Notes\n**9.8.1**  A figure the agent files from what the client reports is the client’s figure. The client is not asked to time a commitment or to keep a log.\n\n**9.8.2**  An estimate becomes observed only when the client says what happened. The agent does not promote it.\n\n**9.8.3**  A commitment at the place the client stays has a travel time of zero. Options 3 and 4 are true for it.\n\n**9.8.4**  No option states which way an estimate is wrong. Folder 01 Time Research found the direction unsettled.\n\n**9.8.5**  The vehicle, the fare, and what travel costs in money are filed in Housing Stability, Transportation.\n\n# 10. Question five: Starting\n**10.1  Measures:** starting what was planned.\n\n**10.2  Asked as:** What happens when it is time to start a commitment on your plan?\n\n**10.3  Progression:** How often a planned commitment starts within the time on the client’s plan, and then what happens when it cannot. The ten levels in order: 1 most not started; 2 started after the delay caused a problem; 3 most started, after the planned time; 4 about half within the planned time; 5 most within the planned time; 6 ready before the planned time; 7 a late start known ahead; 8 a late start dealt with before the time passes; 9 the new time kept; 10 started within the planned time or dealt with ahead, for a month.\n\n## 10.4 Start options\nThe client selects the one that is true of them today.\n\n**1.  I do not start most of what I plan.**\n\nThe plan is made. The start does not follow.\n\n**2.  I start a planned commitment after the delay has caused a problem.**\n\nThe start comes after the problem.\n\n**3.  I start most of what I plan. Most of it starts after the time on my plan.**\n\nThe start happens after the planned time.\n\n**4.  I start about half of what I plan within the time on my plan.**\n\nAbout half starts within the planned time.\n\n**5.  I start most of what I plan within the time on my plan.**\n\nMost starts within the planned time.\n\n**6.  For most of what I plan, I am ready before the time on my plan.**\n\nWhat you need is in place before the start.\n\n**7.  When I cannot start at the time on my plan, I can tell before that time comes.**\n\nYou know ahead that the start will be late.\n\n**8.  When I cannot start at the time on my plan, I deal with it before that time passes.**\n\nA new time is set, or the person who set the time is told.\n\n**9.  When a new time is set, I start at the new time.**\n\nThe new start time is kept.\n\n**10.  For the past month I started what I planned within the time on my plan, or I dealt with a late start before that time passed.**\n\nStarting has worked for a month.\n\n## 10.5 Target options\nEach completes the phrase my target is to. The client selects the one they are aiming for.\n\n**1.**  Deal with a commitment when its time comes.\n\n**2.**  Start a planned commitment when the delay causes a problem.\n\n**3.**  Start most of what is planned, after the time on my plan.\n\n**4.**  Start about half of what is planned within the time on my plan.\n\n**5.**  Start most of what is planned within the time on my plan.\n\n**6.**  Be ready before the time on my plan for most of what is planned.\n\n**7.**  Be able to tell ahead when a start will be late.\n\n**8.**  Deal with a late start before the time on my plan passes.\n\n**9.**  Start at the new time when a new time is set.\n\n**10.**  Start what is planned for a month within the time on my plan, or deal with a late start before that time passes.\n\n## 10.6 What the agent does\n| Start | Where the agent begins |\n| :---- | :---- |\n| 1 to 2 | Asks what has to be in place for one named commitment to start. Offers a reminder before it. Sets the reminder when the client agrees. |\n| 3 to 4 | Asks how far the start can move. Files the answer in the Timing condition. |\n| 5 to 6 | Places the preparation as a block where the client agrees or a standing permission covers it. |\n| 7 to 8 | When the client reports that a start will be late, offers a new time from the open blocks. Sends a message to the person who set the time only on the client’s decision. Files a later start as changed. |\n| 9 to 10 | Does not prompt. Asks about a start only where Folder 01 Time, 7.15, applies. |\n\n## 10.7 Teaching\n**10.7.1**  Lesson 19.17 applies. It is taught when a commitment the client wants to start has no time, no place, or no first step.\n\n## 10.8 Notes\n**10.8.1**  No punctuality rule applies to every client. A start is compared with the time on the client’s plan. Folder 01 Time, 2.6.\n\n**10.8.2**  The time on the plan is the timing the client stated for the commitment. It can be a time the client chose or a time an employer or an institution set.\n\n**10.8.3**  A start time the client cannot move does not limit the scale. Option 8 is met when the person who set the time is told before the time passes.\n\n**10.8.4**  A start that follows a reminder is a start. It counts at every option.\n\n**10.8.5**  A changed start is filed as changed. It is never filed as a failure.\n\n**10.8.6**  A reminder follows the client’s contact preferences. A client who turned reminders off receives none. Folder 01 Time, 5.4.9.\n\n**10.8.7**  About half and most describe a share of what the client planned. A client who plans two commitments and starts both is at most.\n\n# 11. Question six: Completing\n**11.1  Measures:** finishing what was planned.\n\n**11.2  Asked as:** What happens to a commitment on your plan once the day is over?\n\n**11.3  Progression:** How a planned commitment is finished against what the client said finished means, and then what happens to a part that is left. The ten levels in order: 1 most unfinished; 2 finished after leaving it caused a problem; 3 what finished means stated before the start; 4 about half finished as stated; 5 most finished as stated; 6 the part that is left named; 7 an unfinished commitment known before the day ends; 8 the part that is left kept, moved, or dropped by decision the same day; 9 a moved part finished at its new time; 10 finished or decided for a month.\n\n## 11.4 Start options\nThe client selects the one that is true of them today.\n\n**1.  Most of what I plan does not get finished.**\n\nThe plan is not carried out.\n\n**2.  I finish a planned commitment after leaving it has caused a problem.**\n\nThe finish comes after the problem.\n\n**3.  Before I start a commitment, I can say what finished means for it.**\n\nWhat completes it is stated ahead.\n\n**4.  About half of what I plan gets finished the way I said.**\n\nAbout half gets finished as stated.\n\n**5.  Most of what I plan gets finished the way I said.**\n\nMost gets finished as stated.\n\n**6.  When I stop partway, I can say what part is left.**\n\nThe part that is left is named.\n\n**7.  I can tell before the day ends that a commitment will not get finished.**\n\nYou know ahead that a part will be left.\n\n**8.  When a commitment will not get finished, I decide that day to keep, move, or drop the part that is left.**\n\nWhat happens to it is your decision.\n\n**9.  When I move a part that is left, it gets finished at the new time.**\n\nThe new time for the part is kept.\n\n**10.  For the past month what I planned got finished, or I decided what happened to the part that was left.**\n\nCompleting has worked for a month.\n\n## 11.5 Target options\nEach completes the phrase my target is to. The client selects the one they are aiming for.\n\n**1.**  Deal with an unfinished commitment when it comes up.\n\n**2.**  Finish a planned commitment when leaving it causes a problem.\n\n**3.**  Be able to say before starting a commitment what finished means for it.\n\n**4.**  Get about half of what is planned finished the way it was stated.\n\n**5.**  Get most of what is planned finished the way it was stated.\n\n**6.**  Be able to say what part is left when a commitment stops partway.\n\n**7.**  Be able to tell before the day ends that a commitment will not get finished.\n\n**8.**  Decide the same day to keep, move, or drop the part that is left.\n\n**9.**  Finish a moved part at its new time.\n\n**10.**  Get what is planned finished for a month, or decide what happens to the part that is left.\n\n## 11.6 What the agent does\n| Start | Where the agent begins |\n| :---- | :---- |\n| 1 to 2 | Asks what has to happen for one commitment to be fulfilled. Files it as the Completion condition. |\n| 3 to 4 | At Daily Close asks about the commitments that Folder 01 Time, 7.15, names. Compares the outcome with the Completion condition. Files partly executed as partly executed. |\n| 5 to 6 | Asks which part is left. Compares the outcome with a minimum the client named. |\n| 7 to 8 | Asks whether the remaining part is still required. Keeps it, moves it, or removes it as the client decides. Folder 01 Time, 7.4. |\n| 9 to 10 | Does not prompt. Places a moved part at the time the client chose. |\n\n## 11.7 Teaching\n**11.7.1**  Lesson 19.21 applies. It is taught when a commitment is executed and work remains.\n\n## 11.8 Notes\n**11.8.1**  The client states what completes a commitment. The agent does not set it.\n\n**11.8.2**  The scale does not count commitments. A client who plans two and finishes two is at the same option as a client who plans ten and finishes ten.\n\n**11.8.3**  A part the client decides to drop is settled. It does not count against an option.\n\n**11.8.4**  A minimum is filed in Life Management, Standards. Time holds which minimums apply to a commitment.\n\n**11.8.5**  A commitment is marked executed only when the client reports it or evidence on file proves it.\n\n# 12. Question seven: Changes\n**12.1  Measures:** handling a changed plan.\n\n**12.2  Asked as:** What happens to your plan when a commitment changes?\n\n**12.3  Progression:** How much of the plan stays in place after a change, and then how early the plan is changed. The ten levels in order: 1 planning stops for the days that follow; 2 the rest of the day is dropped; 3 the commitments that cannot move stay; 4 the rest of the plan stays and the changed commitment is left open; 5 the changed commitment is settled; 6 the commitments it affects are settled; 7 the changed plan is checked against the time left; 8 the plan is changed before a known change arrives; 9 a second option is set ahead and used; 10 the rest of the plan kept and the changed commitment settled, for a month.\n\n## 12.4 Start options\nThe client selects the one that is true of them today.\n\n**1.  After a change, I stop planning the days that follow.**\n\nOne change ends your planning for longer than that day.\n\n**2.  After a change, I drop the rest of my plan for that day.**\n\nOne change ends the plan for the day.\n\n**3.  After a change, I keep the commitments that cannot move and drop the rest.**\n\nThe fixed ones are kept.\n\n**4.  After a change, I keep the rest of my plan. The changed commitment gets no new time.**\n\nThe rest stays. The changed one is left open.\n\n**5.  After a change, the changed commitment gets a new time, or I decide to drop it.**\n\nThe changed one is settled.\n\n**6.  After a change, I settle the commitments on my plan that it affects.**\n\nThey are moved, kept, or dropped.\n\n**7.  After a change, my plan is checked against the time that is left.**\n\nThe changed plan is checked against the day.\n\n**8.  When I know a change is coming, my plan is changed before it arrives.**\n\nThe plan is changed ahead.\n\n**9.  For a commitment that may not happen, I have a second option set ahead. I use it when the first cannot happen.**\n\nA second option is ready and gets used.\n\n**10.  For the past month a change left the rest of my plan in place. The changed commitment got a new time or was dropped by my decision.**\n\nHandling changes has worked for a month.\n\n## 12.5 Target options\nEach completes the phrase my target is to. The client selects the one they are aiming for.\n\n**1.**  Deal with a change when it comes.\n\n**2.**  Limit a change to the day it happens, and plan again the next day.\n\n**3.**  Keep the commitments that cannot move after a change.\n\n**4.**  Keep the rest of my plan after a change.\n\n**5.**  Give a changed commitment a new time, or decide to drop it.\n\n**6.**  Settle the commitments on my plan that a change affects.\n\n**7.**  Have my plan checked after a change against the time that is left.\n\n**8.**  Have my plan changed before a known change arrives.\n\n**9.**  Have a second option set ahead for a commitment that may not happen, and use it.\n\n**10.**  Keep the rest of my plan in place after a change for a month, and settle the commitment that changed.\n\n## 12.6 What the agent does\n| Start | Where the agent begins |\n| :---- | :---- |\n| 1 to 2 | When the client reports a change, acknowledges it. Asks whether one named commitment is still required today. Does not present the whole Plan. The next day, presents the Plan as usual. |\n| 3 to 4 | States which commitments on the Plan cannot move. Asks what the client wants to do with the rest. |\n| 5 to 6 | Offers a new time for the changed commitment from the open blocks. Moves it when the client agrees or a standing permission covers it. |\n| 7 to 8 | Asks whether the changed Plan fits the time that is left. Updates the schedule ahead when the client reports a change that is coming. |\n| 9 to 10 | Does not prompt. Uses the alternative on file. Acts on a change that affects a stated consequence or a minimum. |\n\n## 12.7 Teaching\n**12.7.1**  Lesson 19.18 applies. It is taught on the day a commitment changes, after the agent has acknowledged the change.\n\n## 12.8 Notes\n**12.8.1**  A changed time alone is never a failure. Folder 01 Time, 11.6.5.\n\n**12.8.2**  The agent acknowledges the change before it raises the rest of the Plan.\n\n**12.8.3**  A schedule the agent or software updates under a standing permission counts at options 5 to 8. The client’s part is to report the change and to decide.\n\n**12.8.4**  A change that an employer, an institution, or a second person made is a change. No option assumes the client chose it.\n\n**12.8.5**  A commitment that an employer or an institution fixed can stay where it is. Option 6 is met when the client decides what happens to the commitments around it.\n\n**12.8.6**  An alternative agreed in advance is filed in its own outcome field. Folder 01 Time, 3.3.10.\n\n# 13. Question eight: Review\n**13.1  Measures:** knowing what happened.\n\n**13.2  Asked as:** At the end of a day, how much can you say about what happened to your plan?\n\n**13.3  Progression:** How much the client can say about what happened to the plan, and then whether it is on file and used. The ten levels in order: 1 no outcome known; 2 the one commitment that did not happen; 3 which commitments happened; 4 which happened, which changed, and which did not happen; 5 when a changed commitment happened; 6 why a commitment changed or did not happen; 7 told or written down the same day; 8 a repeated change known; 9 used in the plan for tomorrow; 10 known and used for a month.\n\n## 13.4 Start options\nThe client selects the one that is true of them today.\n\n**1.  I cannot say what happened to the plan I had yesterday.**\n\nWhat happened to the plan is unknown.\n\n**2.  I remember the commitment that did not happen. I cannot say what happened to the rest.**\n\nOne outcome is known.\n\n**3.  At the end of a day, I can say which of my commitments happened.**\n\nYou know what happened.\n\n**4.  At the end of a day, I can say which commitments happened, which changed, and which did not happen.**\n\nYou can tell the three outcomes apart.\n\n**5.  At the end of a day, I can say when a changed commitment happened.**\n\nThe new time is known.\n\n**6.  At the end of a day, I can say why a commitment changed or did not happen.**\n\nThe reason is known.\n\n**7.  I tell a person who helps me what happened on the same day, or I write it down.**\n\nWhat happened is on file that day.\n\n**8.  I can say when the same commitment has changed more than once.**\n\nYou know when a change repeats.\n\n**9.  What happened today is used in my plan for tomorrow.**\n\nThe next plan uses the last one.\n\n**10.  For the past month I could say what happened to my plans. My next plan used it.**\n\nReview has worked for a month.\n\n## 13.5 Target options\nEach completes the phrase my target is to. The client selects the one they are aiming for.\n\n**1.**  Look at what happened to a plan when a problem comes from it.\n\n**2.**  Remember the commitment that did not happen.\n\n**3.**  Be able to say at the end of a day which commitments happened.\n\n**4.**  Be able to say which commitments happened, which changed, and which did not happen.\n\n**5.**  Be able to say when a changed commitment happened.\n\n**6.**  Be able to say why a commitment changed or did not happen.\n\n**7.**  Tell a person who helps me what happened on the same day, or write it down.\n\n**8.**  Be able to say when the same commitment has changed more than once.\n\n**9.**  Have what happened today used in my plan for tomorrow.\n\n**10.**  Be able to say for a month what happened to my plans, and have my next plan use it.\n\n## 13.6 What the agent does\n| Start | Where the agent begins |\n| :---- | :---- |\n| 1 to 2 | Opens Daily Close with the question Did today go as planned? Asks about a commitment by name. Never asks the client to reconstruct the day. |\n| 3 to 4 | Asks about the commitments that Folder 01 Time, 7.15, names. Files the outcome in the four outcome fields. |\n| 5 to 6 | Asks for the actual time where the timing changed. Asks for a reason only where the client offers one or a consequence is on file. |\n| 7 to 8 | States a repeat with its count and its dates, from outcomes on file. Does not state a cause. |\n| 9 to 10 | Presents the Plan for tomorrow with the changes the client made after earlier outcomes. Does not prompt a review. |\n\n## 13.7 Teaching\n**13.7.1**  Lesson 19.20 applies. It is taught when the time of Daily Close is set, or when the client asks why the agent asks what happened.\n\n**13.7.2**  Lesson 19.15 applies when an estimate and an observed time for one commitment are both on file.\n\n## 13.8 Notes\n**13.8.1**  The agent files the outcomes. The client is not asked to keep a log.\n\n**13.8.2**  A client who answers at Daily Close has told a person who helps them. That counts at option 7.\n\n**13.8.3**  A client who hears a repeat from the agent can say it. That counts at option 8.\n\n**13.8.4**  Daily Close takes place at the time the client chose. The client can report an outcome at any time. Folder 01 Time, 5.4.8.\n\n# 14. Question nine: Consequences\n**14.1  Measures:** what a change affects.\n\n**14.2  Asked as:** When a commitment moves or does not happen, when do you find out what it affects?\n\n**14.3  Progression:** How early the client knows what a moved or missed commitment affects, and then what the client does with it. The ten levels in order: 1 not found out; 2 learned from a person it affected; 3 known afterward; 4 known the same day; 5 known before a move that is coming; 6 known when the commitment is planned; 7 known for which commitments can move with no effect; 8 used in the decision; 9 dealt with the same day; 10 known by the day of the move and dealt with that day, for a month.\n\n## 14.4 Start options\nThe client selects the one that is true of them today.\n\n**1.  I do not find out what a missed or moved commitment affected.**\n\nThe effect is unknown to you.\n\n**2.  I find out what a missed or moved commitment affected when a person it affected tells me.**\n\nYou learn it from that person.\n\n**3.  After a commitment is missed or moved, I can say what it affected.**\n\nYou can name the effect afterward.\n\n**4.  On the day a commitment is missed or moved, I find out what it affects.**\n\nThe effect is known that day.\n\n**5.  When I know a move is coming, I find out what it affects before it happens.**\n\nThe effect is known before the move.\n\n**6.  When I plan a commitment, I can say what it would affect if it moved or did not happen.**\n\nThe effect is known when you plan.\n\n**7.  I can say which of my commitments can move with no effect and which cannot.**\n\nYou know which ones are fixed.\n\n**8.  I use what a move affects when I decide what to do about it.**\n\nThe effect is part of the decision.\n\n**9.  When a move affects a person or a second commitment, I deal with that on the same day.**\n\nThe effect is dealt with that day.\n\n**10.  For the past month I knew what a move affected by the day it happened. I dealt with the effect that day.**\n\nHandling consequences has worked for a month.\n\n## 14.5 Target options\nEach completes the phrase my target is to. The client selects the one they are aiming for.\n\n**1.**  Deal with what a missed commitment affects when it comes up.\n\n**2.**  Find out from the person it affected what a missed or moved commitment affected.\n\n**3.**  Be able to say afterward what a missed or moved commitment affected.\n\n**4.**  Find out what a missed or moved commitment affects on the day it happens.\n\n**5.**  Find out what a known move affects before it happens.\n\n**6.**  Be able to say when planning a commitment what it would affect if it moved or did not happen.\n\n**7.**  Be able to say which commitments can move with no effect and which cannot.\n\n**8.**  Use what a move affects to decide what to do about it.\n\n**9.**  Deal the same day with a move that affects a person or a second commitment.\n\n**10.**  Know for a month what a move affects by the day it happens, and deal with the effect that day.\n\n## 14.6 What the agent does\n| Start | Where the agent begins |\n| :---- | :---- |\n| 1 to 2 | After a missed commitment, asks what it affected only where Folder 01 Time, 11.6.11, applies. Files what the client reports. Leaves Consequences as not established where the client does not know. |\n| 3 to 4 | On the day of a change, states the consequence on file for that commitment and the commitment the new time overlaps. |\n| 5 to 6 | Before a known move, states the consequence on file. Files what the client says a move would affect in the Consequences condition. |\n| 7 to 8 | States the effect of a move and asks the client to decide. Folder 01 Time, 7.8. |\n| 9 to 10 | Does not prompt. Prepares the change to a second commitment. Sends a message to a second person only on the client’s decision. Folder 01 Time, 5.6.7. |\n\n## 14.7 Teaching\n**14.7.1**  Lesson 19.19 applies. It is taught before the client moves a commitment that has a consequence on file.\n\n## 14.8 Notes\n**14.8.1**  A client who hears the consequence on file from the agent has found out. That counts at options 4 to 8.\n\n**14.8.2**  No option assumes the client chose the move. A move that an employer, an institution, or a second person made counts the same. Option 5 covers a move the client was told about ahead.\n\n**14.8.3**  The agent does not invent a consequence. An expected consequence is never filed as a consequence that happened.\n\n**14.8.4**  Not established is never read as none confirmed.\n\n# 15. Question ten: Remaining work\n**15.1  Measures:** work left after.\n\n**15.2  Asked as:** After you do a commitment, what happens to the work that is left?\n\n**15.3  Progression:** How far the work left after a commitment gets, and then whether the client knows what is still open. The ten levels in order: 1 not done; 2 found when it causes a problem; 3 known to exist; 4 named; 5 the client’s part known; 6 given a time; 7 most of the client’s part done by that time; 8 a missed part given a new time the same day; 9 open and finished work known across the client’s responsibilities; 10 given a time and done or set again, for a month.\n\n## 15.4 Start options\nThe client selects the one that is true of them today.\n\n**1.  After I do the main action, the work that is left does not get done.**\n\nThe rest is left undone.\n\n**2.  I find out that work was left when it causes a problem.**\n\nYou learn of it from the problem.\n\n**3.  After a commitment, I can say whether work is left.**\n\nYou know when work remains.\n\n**4.  After a commitment, I can say what work is left.**\n\nThe work that is left is named.\n\n**5.  After a commitment, I can say which part of the work that is left is mine.**\n\nThe work has an owner.\n\n**6.  The work that is left after my commitments has a time set for it.**\n\nIt has a time.\n\n**7.  I do most of my part by the time set for it.**\n\nYour part gets done.\n\n**8.  When my part is not done by the time set, it gets a new time the same day.**\n\nA missed time is set again that day.\n\n**9.  I can say which of my responsibilities still have work open and which are finished.**\n\nYou know what is still open.\n\n**10.  For the past month the work left after my commitments got a time. My part got done or got a new time.**\n\nFollow through has worked for a month.\n\n## 15.5 Target options\nEach completes the phrase my target is to. The client selects the one they are aiming for.\n\n**1.**  Deal with the work left after a commitment when it comes up.\n\n**2.**  Find out about work that was left when it causes a problem.\n\n**3.**  Be able to say whether work is left after a commitment.\n\n**4.**  Be able to say what work is left after a commitment.\n\n**5.**  Be able to say which part of the work that is left is mine.\n\n**6.**  Have a time set for the work that is left after my commitments.\n\n**7.**  Do most of my part by the time set for it.\n\n**8.**  Have a new time set the same day when my part is not done.\n\n**9.**  Be able to say which responsibilities still have work open and which are finished.\n\n**10.**  Have the work left after my commitments get a time for a month, and my part get done or get a new time.\n\n## 15.6 What the agent does\n| Start | Where the agent begins |\n| :---- | :---- |\n| 1 to 2 | After the client reports a commitment, checks the Follow through condition and the linked ongoing work. Opens a follow up for each missing piece. Folder 01 Time, 5.5. |\n| 3 to 4 | States the work that is left from the file. Asks only for a missing piece. |\n| 5 to 6 | Files the owner of each follow up: the client or the agent. Does the agent’s part. Places the work on the schedule where a standing permission covers it. |\n| 7 to 8 | Offers a new time when the client’s part is not done by the time set. |\n| 9 to 10 | Does not prompt. Keeps each follow up open until its requirement is met. |\n\n## 15.7 Teaching\n**15.7.1**  Lesson 19.21 applies. It is taught when a commitment is executed and work remains.\n\n## 15.8 Notes\n**15.8.1**  The agent carries the administrative follow through. This question measures the part that needs the client: information, a decision, a permission, and an action only the client can do. Folder 01 Time, 5.5.6.\n\n**15.8.2**  Work the agent does is done. A client is never asked to repeat it.\n\n**15.8.3**  Work the agent or software places on the schedule under a standing permission has a time. That counts at option 6.\n\n**15.8.4**  A client who hears from the agent what is left can say what is left. That counts at options 3 to 5 and at option 9.\n\n# 16. Question eleven: Recurring responsibilities\n**16.1  Measures:** recurring responsibilities.\n\n**16.2  Asked as:** What happens to the responsibilities that come due again?\n\n**16.3  Progression:** How far ahead of a problem a recurring responsibility is handled, and then how its time or its reminder is kept working. The ten levels in order: 1 done after it caused a problem; 2 done once it is overdue; 3 named; 4 how often it comes due known; 5 one has a time or a reminder; 6 most have a time or a reminder; 7 most done by the time they are due; 8 a missed one given a new time and done; 9 a time or a reminder that stops working changed; 10 done before a problem for a month, with a missed one set again.\n\n## 16.4 Start options\nThe client selects the one that is true of them today.\n\n**1.  I do a recurring responsibility after it has caused a problem.**\n\nThe problem comes first.\n\n**2.  I do a recurring responsibility once I see it is overdue.**\n\nYou act once it is late.\n\n**3.  I can name my recurring responsibilities.**\n\nYou know which ones come back.\n\n**4.  I can say how often my recurring responsibilities come due.**\n\nThe timing is known.\n\n**5.  One of my recurring responsibilities has a time set for it or a reminder.**\n\nOne has a time or a reminder.\n\n**6.  Most of my recurring responsibilities have a time set for them or a reminder.**\n\nMost have a time or a reminder.\n\n**7.  Most of my recurring responsibilities get done by the time they are due.**\n\nMost get done by the time they are due.\n\n**8.  When I miss a recurring responsibility, it gets a new time and gets done.**\n\nOne miss stays one miss.\n\n**9.  When a time or a reminder stops working, I change it or ask for it to be changed.**\n\nThe time or the reminder is kept working.\n\n**10.  For the past month my recurring responsibilities got done before they caused a problem. A missed one got a new time.**\n\nRecurring responsibilities have worked for a month.\n\n## 16.5 Target options\nEach completes the phrase my target is to. The client selects the one they are aiming for.\n\n**1.**  Do a recurring responsibility when it causes a problem.\n\n**2.**  Do a recurring responsibility when it is overdue.\n\n**3.**  Be able to name my recurring responsibilities.\n\n**4.**  Be able to say how often my recurring responsibilities come due.\n\n**5.**  Have a time or a reminder for one recurring responsibility.\n\n**6.**  Have a time or a reminder for most of my recurring responsibilities.\n\n**7.**  Get most of my recurring responsibilities done by the time they are due.\n\n**8.**  Have a missed recurring responsibility get a new time and get done.\n\n**9.**  Change a time or a reminder that stops working, or ask for it to be changed.\n\n**10.**  Get my recurring responsibilities done for a month before they cause a problem, with a missed one given a new time.\n\n## 16.6 What the agent does\n| Start | Where the agent begins |\n| :---- | :---- |\n| 1 to 2 | Asks which responsibility came due most recently and when. Files it as ongoing work. |\n| 3 to 4 | Asks how often a named responsibility comes due. Files the recurrence the client states. Leaves it empty where the client does not know. |\n| 5 to 6 | Places the next commitment on the schedule under the stated recurrence where a standing permission covers it. Offers a reminder. |\n| 7 to 8 | After a miss, offers the next time. Does not raise the miss again. |\n| 9 to 10 | Does not prompt. Keeps the recurrence. Asks only when a recurrence stops fitting the schedule. |\n\n## 16.7 Teaching\n**16.7.1**  Lesson 19.22 applies. It is taught when a recurring responsibility is set up, or after the client misses one.\n\n## 16.8 Notes\n**16.8.1**  The recurrence is the one the client states. No option assumes a weekly cycle or a fixed day.\n\n**16.8.2**  A recurrence the agent or software keeps under a standing permission counts at options 5 to 9. Folder 01 Time Operations, section 6.\n\n**16.8.3**  A responsibility that is done for the client with the client’s permission is done. It counts at option 7.\n\n**16.8.4**  What has to be cleaned and how often is filed in Housing Stability, Sanitation. A bill or a renewal is filed in Housing Stability, Administration.\n\n# 17. Question twelve: Using support\n**17.1  Measures:** use of support.\n\n**17.2  Asked as:** How do you use support to keep your commitments?\n\n**17.3  Progression:** How much the client chooses and directs the support they use. The scale does not measure doing it with no support. The ten levels in order: 1 no support and missed commitments; 2 help asked for after a miss; 3 support that exists and is not used; 4 support chosen by a second person; 5 support the client asks for or agrees to; 6 knowing which commitments need support; 7 knowing which kind works; 8 directing which work is done for the client; 9 changing support that stops working; 10 support that was enough for a month, changed when it was not.\n\n## 17.4 Start options\nThe client selects the one that is true of them today.\n\n**1.  I use memory alone. I miss commitments.**\n\nYour memory is the one place that holds them.\n\n**2.  I ask for help after a commitment has been missed.**\n\nHelp comes after the miss.\n\n**3.  I have a schedule, a list, or a calendar. I do not look at it.**\n\nThe support exists. It is not in use.\n\n**4.  I use support that was chosen for me. I did not choose it.**\n\nThe support is chosen for you.\n\n**5.  I ask for, or agree to, a reminder or help for a commitment I am likely to miss.**\n\nYou choose the support.\n\n**6.  I can say which of my commitments need support and which do not.**\n\nYou know where you need it.\n\n**7.  I can say which kind of support works for me.**\n\nYou know which kind you need.\n\n**8.  I tell my agent which work I want done for me and which work I want to do.**\n\nYou decide what is done for you.\n\n**9.  When a kind of support stops working, I change it or ask for it to be changed.**\n\nYour support is kept working.\n\n**10.  For the past month the support I used was enough to keep my commitments. I changed it when it was not.**\n\nYour support has worked for a month.\n\n## 17.5 Target options\nEach completes the phrase my target is to. The client selects the one they are aiming for.\n\n**1.**  Deal with a need for support when it comes up.\n\n**2.**  Ask for help when a commitment has been missed.\n\n**3.**  Have a schedule, a list, or a calendar.\n\n**4.**  Use support that was chosen for me.\n\n**5.**  Ask for, or agree to, a reminder or help for a commitment likely to be missed.\n\n**6.**  Be able to say which commitments need support and which do not.\n\n**7.**  Be able to say which kind of support works for me.\n\n**8.**  Tell my agent which work to do for me and which work is mine to do.\n\n**9.**  Change a kind of support that stops working, or ask for it to be changed.\n\n**10.**  Have support for a month that is enough to keep my commitments, and change it when it is not.\n\n## 17.6 What the agent does\n| Start | Where the agent begins |\n| :---- | :---- |\n| 1 to 2 | Offers one reminder for the next commitment. Sets it when the client agrees. |\n| 3 to 4 | Asks which kind of reminder the client reads. Uses that kind. |\n| 5 to 6 | Asks which commitments the client wants a reminder for. Files the contact preference. |\n| 7 to 8 | Asks which routine work the client wants done under a standing permission. Files the permission in the words the client used. |\n| 9 to 10 | Does not prompt. Changes a reminder or a permission when the client asks. |\n\n## 17.7 Teaching\n**17.7.1**  Lesson 19.23 applies. It is taught when the client holds back from asking for a reminder, or when a commitment was missed and no reminder was set.\n\n## 17.8 Notes\n**17.8.1**  No option places memory alone above support. Support does not lower an assessment. More support is never a lower option. Guided Life is support.\n\n**17.8.2**  Target 1 states no way of keeping commitments. It describes dealing with a need for support when the need comes up.\n\n**17.8.3**  Options 4 and 5 differ by who chose the support. A reminder the agent offers and the client agrees to is the client’s choice. It counts at option 5.\n\n**17.8.4**  Option 1 states two facts: memory alone, and missed commitments. A client who keeps commitments by memory alone and misses none selects the option that is true of them.\n\n**17.8.5**  A reminder the agent sets is a reminder. The client is not asked to set it.\n\n**17.8.6**  A client who turns a reminder off has made a choice about support. The agent follows it. Folder 01 Time, 5.4.9.\n\n**17.8.7**  The settings for contact and the list of standing permissions are in Folder 01 Time Operations, sections 6 and 7.\n\n# 18. Schedule intake\n**18.1**  Intake is the first stage of the arc. The agent establishes what already has the client’s time, assembles the first seven day schedule, and makes the first Plan with the client. Folder 01 Time, 9.3.\n\n**18.2**  Intake starts after Orientation. The client does not build the schedule during Enrollment. Folder 01 Time, 9.2.\n\n**18.3**  The client is never asked to fill an empty grid. The agent assembles the schedule from what the client says in conversation and from what is already on file. Folder 01 Time, 2.4.1.\n\n**18.4**  The twelve questions are answered before intake. Intake asks no assessment question. It collects what the schedule needs.\n\n## 18.5 What the first seven day schedule needs\n**18.5.1**  The first seven day schedule holds the client’s recurring commitments, with their travel and preparation filed as costs of them. Folder 01 Time, 10.2.\n\n**18.5.2**  The agent needs five facts for each recurring commitment.\n\n| Fact | What the agent establishes | Rule |\n| :---- | :---- | :---- |\n| The commitment and its days | Each commitment that comes back in the client’s usual week, and the days it falls on | A commitment already on file is read from the file |\n| Its time | The start and the end, or the usual length | Filed as estimated until the client states what happened. Folder 01 Time, 3.6.1 and 3.6.2 |\n| Its travel and its preparation | How the client gets there and back, and how long getting ready takes | Filed as costs of the commitment. A trip is counted both ways |\n| Whether its timing can move | Which commitments are fixed, and how far each of the rest can move | Filed in the Timing condition of the commitment |\n| Paid, unpaid, or neither | Whether the client is paid for the hours | Asked once for each new kind of work. Folder 01 Time, 6.13 |\n\n**18.5.3**  Recovery after a commitment is filed as a cost of that commitment where the client states it. Folder 01 Time, 3.2.4.\n\n**18.5.4**  Intake ends with the first Plan. The first Plan asks how realistic the Plan feels and what time Daily Close belongs in the client’s day. Folder 01 Time, 5.3.3 and 5.3.4.\n\n**18.5.5**  The Resident Portal home opens when the first seven day schedule is on file and the client has confirmed the first Plan. A completed week of activity is not required. Folder 01 Time, 9.3.\n\n## 18.6 What the agent does not ask\n**18.6.1  A fact already on file.** The agent reads the application, the Enrollment answers, and each earlier conversation before the agent asks.\n\n**18.6.2  A kind of time the client’s week does not hold.** No kind of time is required. A schedule with no block of paid work is a valid schedule. Language Standard 6.5.3.\n\n**18.6.3  A whole day from memory.** The agent asks about one named block. Folder 01 Time, 5.4.7 and 6.12.\n\n**18.6.4  A week of observed time.** The first schedule can hold estimates. Each estimate stays marked as an estimate. Folder 01 Time, 3.6.7.\n\n**18.6.5  The subject of the hours.** Time holds the hours and the commitment. The subject is filed in the folder that Folder 01 Time, section 4, names.\n\n**18.6.6  A typical day to fill a gap.** An hour the client has not accounted for stays unaccounted. Folder 01 Time, 8.6.\n\n## 18.7 The eleven kinds of time\n**18.7.1**  Version 1 asked one question for each of twelve subjects. Eleven of them are the kinds of block on the schedule. Folder 01 Time Operations, 2.1.2.\n\n**18.7.2**  The agent uses the eleven kinds as a check after the client has described the week. The agent does not go through the eleven with the client.\n\n**18.7.3**  The agent asks about a kind of time in three cases: a commitment on file requires it, the client named it, or a different folder produced it. The third column states the case for each kind.\n\n| Kind of time | What the agent establishes | The agent asks when | The subject is filed in |\n| :---- | :---- | :---- | :---- |\n| Sleep | The hours the client is usually asleep | A commitment falls in hours the client said they sleep, or the client names sleep | Agency Assessment, Health, for a sleep problem |\n| Getting ready | How long getting ready takes before a commitment | A commitment has a start time that cannot move | Housing Stability, Inventory, for what the client uses to get ready |\n| Travel | The trip to a commitment and the trip back | A commitment is on file with no travel attached. Folder 01 Time, 7.9 | Housing Stability, Transportation, for the vehicle and the fare |\n| Paid work | The hours worked. The hours scheduled are a second fact | The client has paid work | Career Development, Schedule, for the shift. Career Development, Salary, for the pay |\n| Unpaid work time | The preparation, the travel, and the recovery that a shift requires | A shift is on file | No second folder. Each block is a cost of that shift |\n| Household time | The recurring work of the place the client stays, with the recurrence the client states | The client names the work, or a different folder produces it | Housing Stability: Sanitation, Organization, or Administration |\n| Caring for people | The hours and the relationship | The client names a person who relies on them | Agency Assessment, Background, for the person |\n| Appointments | The date, the time, the travel, and the waiting | The client reports an appointment, or a different folder produces one | Health, Legal, or Administration, for the matter |\n| Time with people | The hours | The client plans the time or reports it | Life Management, Socializing |\n| Rest and leisure | The hours. Rest counts the same as every other commitment | The client plans the time or reports it | Agency Assessment, Regulation |\n| Time on what you are building | The block the client claims for their own work | The client names what they are building | The Career Development or Education folder for that work |\n\n**18.7.4**  A third party’s medical, financial, or legal detail is never filed. The agent files the hours and the relationship. Language Standard 10.7.3.\n\n**18.7.5**  Care given through an organization is filed in Agency Assessment, Community. Contact for work is filed in Career Development, Networking.\n\n## 18.8 Control over your week\n**18.8.1**  The twelfth subject of Version 1 was control over your week. It is kept as two facts and one figure.\n\n**18.8.2  Which commitments cannot move.** Asked for each recurring commitment. Filed in the Timing condition.\n\n**18.8.3  How far ahead the client learns the times.** Asked only for a commitment whose times change from week to week. Filed as the client’s statement. Folder 01 Time Operations defines no field for it. Section 22 holds the question.\n\n**18.8.4  The hours the client controls.** The schedule produces this figure once a week of observed time is on file. It is calculated from observed time only. It is not a requirement of intake. Folder 01 Time, 3.6.3, 10.6, and 11.5.\n\n## 18.9 Rules kept from Version 1\n**18.9.1**  A trip is counted both ways.\n\n**18.9.2**  The hours worked are filed. A shift that ran forty minutes late is filed forty minutes longer.\n\n**18.9.3**  A time the client states as what happened is observed. A usual amount is estimated. The agent asks which one it is. Folder 01 Time, 6.11.\n\n**18.9.4**  A block that exists because of a commitment is filed as a cost of that commitment.\n\n**18.9.5**  Unpaid work on the client’s own business or project is filed as time on what you are building.\n\n**18.9.6**  Household work done for a person who relies on the client is filed as caring for people.\n\n**18.9.7**  A schedule with no block of time on what you are building is a valid schedule.\n\n**18.9.8**  A reminder for an appointment is set in the schedule. Time owns the calendar for the whole program. Folder 01 Time, 11.4.\n\n## 18.10 Where the fields are held\n**18.10.1**  The fields of a block, the eleven kinds, and the block statuses are held in Folder 01 Time Operations, 2.1. The first Plan is described in Folder 01 Time Operations, 4.2. This document repeats neither.\n\n# 19. The teaching layer\n**19.1**  The teaching layer holds fourteen lessons. Each lesson is a concept under the folder, taught with an entry from the client’s own file. Language Standard 8.3.2.\n\n**19.2**  The lessons have no fixed order. A lesson is taught when the client’s file holds the entry the lesson needs and the lesson is useful to a decision the client is making.\n\n**19.3**  No lesson is a condition for the schedule, for a Plan, or for a grade. Version 1 ran six lessons after the twelve questions and before the schedule was built. That order is retired.\n\n**19.4**  A lesson can be delivered in a guided session. It is delivered at a point where no reply is needed, or at a move from one step to the next. Folder 01 Time, 5.12.13.\n\n**19.5**  What a lesson says and its source are identical for every client. Its length, its depth, and its timing follow the client’s stated preference and the lessons already delivered. Language Standard 8.3.2.\n\n**19.6**  This document holds the fourteen lessons about time. A lesson about a different subject is held by the Curriculum of the folder that owns it. Where that folder has no approved Curriculum, no lesson is delivered.\n\n**19.7**  Each lesson has five parts: what it says, when it is taught, what the agent shows from the client’s file, where it comes from, and its kind of support.\n\n**19.8**  A lesson has one of five kinds of support. The first four are defined in Folder 01 Time Research, verification entry of October 11, 2026, section 4.\n\n**19.8.1  Supports the practice.** A study tested the same practice in a second setting.\n\n**19.8.2  Analogy.** A study tested a different task or a different group of people. The finding is applied to Time by inference.\n\n**19.8.3  Framework.** A way to organize the subject. No test stands behind its use in Time.\n\n**19.8.4  Association.** Two measures move together. No cause is shown.\n\n**19.8.5  Program position.** Universe City’s own operating principle. No study establishes it.\n\n**19.9**  No finding below was tested inside Universe City.\n\n## 19.10 A week has 168 hours\n**19.10.1  What it says.** A week has 168 hours. Each use of time takes hours from that total.\n\n**19.10.2  When it is taught.** A week of the schedule is on file and the client is deciding on a new commitment.\n\n**19.10.3  From the client’s file.** The agent states three figures for one week beside 168: the observed hours, the estimated hours, and the unaccounted hours.\n\n**19.10.4  Where it comes from.** The figure is arithmetic. Seven days of 24 hours make 168. Seneca wrote On the Shortness of Life about the year 49. He observed that people guard their property and let other people take their time. Folder 01 Time Research did not check this source again on October 11, 2026.\n\n**19.10.5  Kind of support.** Program position.\n\nSource: [Seneca, On the Shortness of Life, full text](https://archive.org/stream/SenecaOnTheShortnessOfLife/Seneca+on+the+Shortness+of+Life_djvu.txt)\n\n## 19.11 An hour that is not accounted for is not counted as free\n**19.11.1  What it says.** An hour with no entry on the schedule has no answer yet. The agent does not count it as free time.\n\n**19.11.2  When it is taught.** A week of the schedule is on file, it holds unaccounted hours, and the client is planning a commitment in those hours.\n\n**19.11.3  From the client’s file.** The agent states the unaccounted hours for the week and the days they fall on. The hours the client controls are calculated from observed time only. Folder 01 Time, 3.6.3.\n\n**19.11.4  Where it comes from.** This is Universe City’s operating principle. Research on time use sorts a day into four kinds of time: necessary, contracted, committed, and free. No study establishes a healthy number of free hours. The agent states no threshold.\n\n**19.11.5  Kind of support.** Program position. The four kinds of time are a framework.\n\nSource: [Ås, Studies of time-use: problems and prospects, 1978](https://www.timeuse.org/sites/ctur/files/public/ctur_journal_article/2179/DagfinnAs.pdf)\n\n## 19.12 The longest unbroken block\n**19.12.1  What it says.** The schedule produces two figures: the hours a week the client controls, and the longest unbroken block. Both are stated. A commitment that needs one long period is planned against the second figure.\n\n**19.12.2  When it is taught.** A week of observed time is on file and the client is planning a commitment that needs one long period.\n\n**19.12.3  From the client’s file.** The agent states both figures and the day the longest unbroken block falls on. Folder 01 Time, 11.5.\n\n**19.12.4  Where it comes from.** This is Universe City’s operating principle. Folder 01 Time Research found no study that compares one unbroken block with the same total time in pieces. A study of 35,375 Americans found that wellbeing changes with the amount of discretionary time, so the total holds information too. Mark, Gonzalez and Harris observed 24 workers. Interrupted work was resumed after 25 minutes 26 seconds on average, with other work in between. That figure is the time before the worker returned to the work.\n\n**19.12.5  Kind of support.** Program position.\n\nSource: [Sharif, Mogilner and Hershfield, Having too little or too much time is linked to lower subjective well-being, 2021](https://doi.org/10.1037/pspp0000391)\n\nSource: [Mark, Gonzalez and Harris, No task left behind? Examining the nature of fragmented work, 2005](https://doi.org/10.1145/1054972.1055017)\n\n## 19.13 A new commitment takes time from another use\n**19.13.1  What it says.** Each hour given to a new commitment comes from another use of that hour.\n\n**19.13.2  When it is taught.** The client is deciding on a new commitment and the schedule holds an entry in that time.\n\n**19.13.3  From the client’s file.** The agent states what is on the schedule in that time. Where the commitment takes the controlled hours figure below the client’s target, the agent states the figure before and after. The agent can recommend where the commitment fits. The client decides whether to take it. Folder 01 Time, 7.13.\n\n**19.13.4  Where it comes from.** Economics calls this opportunity cost. The cost of a choice is the best alternative given up for it. No study is cited for this lesson.\n\n**19.13.5  Kind of support.** Framework.\n\n## 19.14 A commitment takes more time than its scheduled hours\n**19.14.1  What it says.** A commitment includes the time to get ready, the travel both ways, and the time needed after it. Those hours come from the same day.\n\n**19.14.2  When it is taught.** A commitment with its travel is on file and the client is planning the same commitment again.\n\n**19.14.3  From the client’s file.** The agent states the scheduled hours of one commitment beside the total with its costs attached. Both figures are the client’s own.\n\n**19.14.4  Where it comes from.** This is Universe City’s operating principle. The American Time Use Survey of the Bureau of Labor Statistics codes travel by its purpose. Travel to work is coded as travel related to work. Two systems that agree do not show the design fits Time.\n\n**19.14.5  Kind of support.** Program position. The survey coding is a framework.\n\nSource: [US Bureau of Labor Statistics, American Time Use Survey, 2025 Results](https://www.bls.gov/news.release/pdf/atus.pdf)\n\n## 19.15 An estimate and an outcome are two figures\n**19.15.1  What it says.** The time a client expects a commitment to take and the time it took are two figures. Both stay on file. The client compares them.\n\n**19.15.2  When it is taught.** An estimate and an observed time for one commitment are both on file.\n\n**19.15.3  From the client’s file.** The agent states the two figures for that commitment. The agent does not state which way the client’s estimates run.\n\n**19.15.4  Where it comes from.** Buehler, Griffin and Ross asked 37 students when they would finish a thesis. The students predicted 33.9 days and took 55.5. Fewer than one third, 29.7 percent, finished by the date they predicted. That study covered one long piece of work. The direction of the error for shorter work is unsettled. One review reports that work under five minutes is more often overestimated. A later review questions that finding.\n\n**19.15.5  Kind of support.** Analogy.\n\nSource: [Buehler, Griffin and Ross, Exploring the planning fallacy, 1994](https://doi.org/10.1037/0022-3514.67.3.366)\n\nSource: [Roy, Christenfeld and McKenzie, Underestimating the duration of future events, 2005](https://doi.org/10.1037/0033-2909.131.5.738)\n\nSource: [Halkjelsvik and Jørgensen, From origami to software development, 2012](https://doi.org/10.1037/a0025996)\n\n## 19.16 A Plan fits the hours in the day\n**19.16.1  What it says.** A Plan holds the commitments the client can carry out in the hours that day has. A Plan the client calls unlikely is made smaller or moved before it is saved.\n\n**19.16.2  When it is taught.** Before a Plan is saved, when the commitments on it take more time than the open hours of that day.\n\n**19.16.3  From the client’s file.** The agent states the time the commitments on the Plan take, with their costs, beside the open hours of that day. The agent asks how realistic the Plan feels. Folder 01 Time, 5.3.3.\n\n**19.16.4  Where it comes from.** This is Universe City’s operating principle. A Plan is never graded by how many commitments it holds. Folder 01 Time, 2.7.\n\n**19.16.5  Kind of support.** Program position.\n\n## 19.17 A plan names when, where, and how\n**19.17.1  What it says.** A plan that names when the action happens, where it happens, and how it starts improved outcomes in research.\n\n**19.17.2  When it is taught.** The client names a commitment they want to carry out, and the commitment has no time, no place, or no first step.\n\n**19.17.3  From the client’s file.** The agent states the commitment as it is on file and names the missing piece. The agent asks for that piece.\n\n**19.17.4  Where it comes from.** A review counted 642 tests of a plan in the form “if this happens, I will do that.” The effects ran from d \\= .27 to d \\= .66. The effect was larger when the person wanted the outcome and rehearsed the plan.\n\n**19.17.5  Kind of support.** Supports the practice.\n\nSource: [Sheeran, Listrom and Gollwitzer, The when and how of planning, 2025](https://doi.org/10.1080/10463283.2024.2334563)\n\n## 19.18 A plan can change\n**19.18.1  What it says.** A plan that changes is still a plan. One commitment can move, get shorter, or come off the Plan while the remaining commitments stay. A changed time alone is never a failure.\n\n**19.18.2  When it is taught.** On the day a commitment changes, after the agent has acknowledged the change.\n\n**19.18.3  From the client’s file.** The agent states the commitments on that day’s Plan that are still planned. Where an alternative is on file, the agent states it.\n\n**19.18.4  Where it comes from.** This is Universe City’s operating principle. Folder 01 Time, 11.6.5. Brad Aeon and Herman Aguinis define time management as “a form of decision making used by individuals to structure, protect, and adapt their time to changing conditions.” Folder 01 Time Research holds the quotation.\n\n**19.18.5  Kind of support.** Program position. The definition is a framework.\n\n## 19.19 A change can affect a second commitment\n**19.19.1  What it says.** A commitment that moves or does not happen can affect a second commitment, a second person, or a deadline. The effect is checked before the commitment moves.\n\n**19.19.2  When it is taught.** Before the client moves a commitment that has a consequence on file, or when the new time overlaps a second commitment.\n\n**19.19.3  From the client’s file.** The agent states the consequence the client stated for that commitment, or the commitment the new time overlaps. The agent asks the client to decide. Folder 01 Time, 7.8.\n\n**19.19.4  Where it comes from.** This is Universe City’s operating principle. The agent states a consequence only where the client stated it. Folder 01 Time, 8.10.\n\n**19.19.5  Kind of support.** Program position.\n\n## 19.20 Progress that is written down and reported\n**19.20.1  What it says.** Checking progress on a commitment improved results in research. The effect was larger when the progress was written down and when it was reported to another person.\n\n**19.20.2  When it is taught.** The time of Daily Close is being set, or the client asks why the agent asks what happened.\n\n**19.20.3  From the client’s file.** The agent states which commitments on the past seven Plans have an outcome on file and which have none.\n\n**19.20.4  Where it comes from.** Harkin and others reviewed 138 randomized studies with 19,951 people. A prompt to monitor progress improved the outcome, d \\= 0.40. After the authors adjusted for unpublished studies the effect was d \\= 0.19. The effect was d \\= 0.47 when progress was reported to another person and d \\= 0.19 when it was kept private. It was d \\= 0.43 when progress was written down and d \\= 0.29 when it was not. All but one of the studies concerned health.\n\n**19.20.5  Daily Close.** Daily Close is a program design that follows from this finding. No study has tested Daily Close. The agent does not describe Daily Close to a client as tested.\n\n**19.20.6  Kind of support.** Supports the practice of monitoring progress. Daily Close is a design inference.\n\nSource: [Harkin and others, Does monitoring goal progress promote goal attainment?, accepted manuscript, 2016](https://eprints.whiterose.ac.uk/id/eprint/87431/)\n\n## 19.21 Doing the action and finishing the responsibility are two facts\n**19.21.1  What it says.** A commitment can be done while work it belongs to is still open. The commitment stays done. The work that is left gets its own time.\n\n**19.21.2  Steps.** Where a commitment has steps, the same idea holds one level down. A reported step is one part of the commitment. Folder 01 Time, 3.3.21.\n\n**19.21.3  When it is taught.** A commitment is executed and work remains.\n\n**19.21.4  From the client’s file.** The agent states the executed commitment, each open follow up with its owner, and the ongoing work the commitment belongs to.\n\n**19.21.5  Where it comes from.** This is Universe City’s operating principle. Folder 01 Time, 3.3.12.\n\n**19.21.6  Kind of support.** Program position.\n\n## 19.22 A recurring action takes time to become automatic\n**19.22.1  What it says.** A recurring action is repeated for weeks or months before a person does it with no decision each time. One missed day did not change the result in the study below.\n\n**19.22.2  When it is taught.** A recurring responsibility is being set up, or the client missed one.\n\n**19.22.3  From the client’s file.** The agent states the recurrence the client stated and the dates the responsibility was done. After a miss, the agent states the date of the next one.\n\n**19.22.4  Where it comes from.** Lally and others followed 96 volunteers who each repeated one daily action for 12 weeks. The time for the action to become automatic ran from 18 to 254 days. Missing one day did not change the result. The study covered one small daily action. It is applied to a recurring responsibility by inference.\n\n**19.22.5  Kind of support.** Analogy.\n\nSource: [Lally and others, How are habits formed, 2010](https://doi.org/10.1002/ejsp.674)\n\n## 19.23 A reminder is support\n**19.23.1  What it says.** A reminder, a schedule, a list, a calendar, an agent, Guided Life, and a person who helps are support. Using support does not lower an assessment. Knowing which commitments need support is an ability.\n\n**19.23.2  When it is taught.** The client holds back from asking for a reminder, or a commitment was missed and no reminder was set for it.\n\n**19.23.3  From the client’s file.** The agent states which commitments on the Plan have a reminder set and which have none. The agent offers one reminder.\n\n**19.23.4  Where it comes from.** In a Cochrane review, attendance at health appointments was 67.8 percent with no reminder and 78.6 percent with a text reminder. In a review of 16 trials with 2,742 patients, text messages raised the odds of taking medication as prescribed, with an odds ratio of 2.11. Both reviews cover health care.\n\n**19.23.5  Two further sources.** Gilbert and others found that people set more reminders than was best for them in a memory task of about one minute. That finding is an analogy. The World Health Organization classification keeps apart what a person does with no assistance and what a person does with the devices and the people who assist them. That distinction is a framework.\n\n**19.23.6  Kind of support.** Supports the practice for a reminder before a commitment. The rule that support does not lower an assessment is a program position.\n\nSource: [Cochrane review, Mobile phone messaging reminders for attendance at healthcare appointments, 2013](https://doi.org/10.1002/14651858.CD007458.pub3)\n\nSource: [Thakkar and others, Mobile telephone text messaging for medication adherence in chronic disease, 2016](https://doi.org/10.1001/jamainternmed.2015.7667)\n\nSource: [Gilbert and others, Optimal use of reminders, 2020](https://doi.org/10.1037/xge0000652)\n\nSource: [World Health Organization, Towards a Common Language for Functioning, Disability and Health, 2002](https://cdn.who.int/media/docs/default-source/classification/icf/icfbeginnersguide.pdf)\n\n## 19.24 Statements of Version 1 that are withdrawn\n**19.24.1**  Each statement below was in a Version 1 lesson. No lesson in Version 2 makes it. Folder 01 Time Research holds the reason for each.\n\n**19.24.2  An eight hour shift is a ten or eleven hour commitment.** No source gives this figure. The lesson in 19.14 uses the client’s own two figures.\n\n**19.24.3  The gap is almost always larger than they expect.** No source gives this. The lesson in 19.15 states no direction.\n\n**19.24.4  Total free hours is close to useless as a figure.** The total holds information. The lesson in 19.12 states both figures.\n\n**19.24.5  A worker takes around twenty five minutes to return after an interruption.** The figure was misdescribed. The lesson in 19.12 states what the study measured.\n\n**19.24.6  A person needs at least ten hours a week of discretionary time.** Clair Vickery did not propose this figure. The lesson in 19.11 states no threshold.\n\n**19.24.7  People underestimate a task they have done many times.** The statement is too broad. The lesson in 19.15 reports one study of one long piece of work.\n\n**19.24.8  Attention residue.** Folder 01 Time Research did not check this source on October 11, 2026. No lesson cites it.\n\n# 20. Teaching constraints\n**20.1**  A lesson is taught with an entry from the client’s own file. A lesson with no entry from the client’s file is not taught.\n\n**20.2**  The agent states the figure and the source. The agent does not state what the figure means about the client. Language Standard 6.3.\n\n**20.3**  Each lesson states its kind of support. The agent does not present a program position as a research finding.\n\n**20.4**  The teaching layer is identical for every client. The entries it is taught with are the client’s own. Language Standard 8.3.2.\n\n**20.5**  A lesson follows the rules in section 4. The agent acknowledges the client’s present subject first. A lesson is short.\n\n**20.6**  The style of delivery changes no part of what a lesson says. Language Standard 4.13.4.\n\n**20.7**  No lesson is a condition for the schedule, for a Plan, or for a grade.\n\n**20.8**  No lesson states that planning improves wellbeing or performance. A review of 158 studies found time management related to wellbeing at r \\= .313 and to job performance at r \\= .259. Those figures show an association. They do not show a cause.\n\n**20.9**  No lesson promises an outcome. Language Standard 10.8.4.\n\n**20.10**  No lesson asks the client to keep a log or to track time.\n\n**20.11**  No lesson compares the client with a second client or with an average. Language Standard 6.3.3.\n\n**20.12**  A figure from a study describes the people in that study. The agent states it as a finding about that study.\n\n# 21. Sources\nFolder 01 Time Research holds the evidence, what was read, and what is still unverified. This list names each source a lesson cites.\n\n* Seneca, On the Shortness of Life, about the year 49.\n\n* Ås (1978). Studies of time-use: problems and prospects.\n\n* US Bureau of Labor Statistics. American Time Use Survey, 2025 Results.\n\n* Sharif, Mogilner and Hershfield (2021). Having too little or too much time is linked to lower subjective well-being.\n\n* Mark, Gonzalez and Harris (2005). No task left behind? Examining the nature of fragmented work.\n\n* Buehler, Griffin and Ross (1994). Exploring the planning fallacy.\n\n* Roy, Christenfeld and McKenzie (2005). Underestimating the duration of future events.\n\n* Halkjelsvik and Jørgensen (2012). From origami to software development.\n\n* Sheeran, Listrom and Gollwitzer (2025). The when and how of planning.\n\n* Harkin and others (2016). Does monitoring goal progress promote goal attainment?\n\n* Lally and others (2010). How are habits formed.\n\n* Cochrane review (2013). Mobile phone messaging reminders for attendance at healthcare appointments.\n\n* Thakkar and others (2016). Mobile telephone text messaging for medication adherence in chronic disease.\n\n* Gilbert and others (2020). Optimal use of reminders.\n\n* World Health Organization (2002). Towards a Common Language for Functioning, Disability and Health.\n\n* Aeon, Faber and Panaccio (2021). Does time management work? A meta-analysis.\n\n* Aeon and Aguinis. The definition of time management, as quoted in Folder 01 Time Research.\n\n# 22. Open items\n**22.1**  Six points that an earlier draft of this section listed as conflicts were settled on October 11, 2026. Language Standard 3.20.4, 8.3.2, 8.3.3, 9.6, 9.8, and 9.8.1 now state the rule this document follows. Folder 01 Time, 5.9.2, now lists five factors. Folder 01 Time Developments, 2.20 to 2.27, holds the record.\n\n**22.2  Language Standard 9.5.** Clause 9.5 requires a question set derived from established measurement. The twelve are drawn from research on planning, progress monitoring, and reminders. No validated instrument measures the twelve. Is that derivation enough for clause 9.5? Language Standard 12.15 holds the same question.\n\n**22.3  Answering twice.** Is each question answered twice: once for what the client does alone, and once for what the client does with the schedule and the agent? This document asks once and counts support at every option. No tested scale exists for answering twice.\n\n**22.4  A second Start.** The Enrollment Start is kept. Does the client select a second Start after a period of outcomes? How long is that period?\n\n**22.5  Ten options.** Are the ten options tested with clients before this document is final? Whether a client can tell ten levels apart is unknown.\n\n**22.6  Twelve, three, or one.** Are the twelve questions twelve abilities, three, or one? The answer needs answers from clients.\n\n**22.7  Enrollment.** How many of the twelve questions does a client answer before Enrollment ends?\n\n**22.8  Notice.** Where is the fact in 18.8 filed: how far ahead a client learns the times of a commitment that changes from week to week?\n\n**22.9  Six terms and one instruction.** The definitions of commitment, plan, set, take on, recurring responsibility, and support in 5.4, and the instruction that follows them, are new client copy. Are they approved?\n\n**22.10  The repository.** This document is a Word file. Is it added to the repository when Version 2 is approved?\n\nGoverned by the Language Standard, section 9, for question composition.\n"},{"key":"research","title":"Folder 01 Time Research","path":"docs/systems/TIME_RESEARCH.md","text":"**Folder 01: Time, Research**\n\n*Department I, Agency Assessment. Living document. Last changed October 11, 2026. The evidence and reasoning behind the Time folder.*\n\nA running list. New research is added as a dated entry. Nothing is removed. When a finding is superseded, the newer entry says so and the older entry stays.\n\nThe specification it supports is Folder 01 Time.\n\n# Entry: 28 August 2026\nSource material is the American Time Use Survey from the Bureau of Labor Statistics, the United Nations International Classification of Activities for Time-Use Statistics, and the academic literature on time poverty and discretionary time.\n\n## 1. The finding that changes the pitch\nThe 2025 American Time Use Survey shows the average American aged 15 and over has about 5.2 hours a day of leisure and sports time. A separate analysis of the same dataset found no demographic subgroup averaging under 4.5 hours of free time a day.\n\nOf that leisure time, 2.6 hours a day goes to watching television. Half of it.\n\nLower income and lower education groups reported more free time than college educated higher income groups, not less, and spent more of it on screens.\n\nThis matters because the folder as first written implied the client has no time and we will find it. On the numbers, most people already have several hours a day. What they do not have is any idea where it goes, and it arrives in pieces they cannot use.\n\nSo the honest promise is not that we will find them time. It is that we will show them the time they already have, in usable pieces, so they can decide what it goes to. That is the stronger claim anyway, because it can be proved inside a month.\n\nSource: [US Bureau of Labor Statistics, American Time Use Survey 2025 results](https://www.bls.gov/news.release/atus.htm)\n\n## 2. The structure the field uses\nTime use research splits a day into three parts, and that split is the basis for every serious measure in the field.\n\n**Necessary time.** Sleep, eating, personal care and grooming. Not negotiable.\n\n**Committed time.** Paid work, plus unpaid domestic labor and caregiving. Obligations that are real even though nobody schedules them for you. Within this the field separates contracted time, meaning paid work, from committed time, meaning household and care.\n\n**Discretionary time.** What is left after the first two.\n\nDiscretionary time is the exact quantity this folder calls the hours a client controls. That number is not something we invented. It has a literature, thresholds, and decades of measurement behind it.\n\nClair Vickery, an American economist, proposed in 1977 that a person needs at least ten hours a week of discretionary time. Later researchers have used two hours a day, or a proportion of the population median, instead. There is no single agreed figure, which means we can pick a defensible one and say why.\n\nSource: [Time poverty thresholds in the USA](https://link.springer.com/rwe/10.1007/978-94-007-0753-5_3704)\n\n## 3. Two classification systems worth taking from\nThe Bureau of Labor Statistics is the United States federal agency that measures employment, prices, and how people spend their days. Since 2003 it has run the American Time Use Survey, which codes activities into 17 major lexicon categories, published as roughly a dozen: personal care, eating and drinking, household activities, purchasing goods and services, caring for household members, caring for non household members, working and work related, education, organizational and civic and religious, leisure and sports, telephone and mail and email, and a residual.\n\nThe United Nations Statistics Division maintains the International Classification of Activities for Time-Use Statistics, adopted in 2017. It uses nine major divisions: employment, production of goods for own use, unpaid domestic services, unpaid caregiving, unpaid volunteer and trainee work, learning, socializing and community and religious practice, culture and leisure and media and sports, and self care and maintenance.\n\nIts stated design principles are worth copying directly. Categories must be mutually exclusive and exhaustive, stable, well described, and well balanced, meaning not too many and not too few.\n\n**The most useful detail.** The American survey codes travel according to the purpose of the travel. Driving to work is coded as travel related to working, not as a separate transport category. That is structurally identical to the cost of field in this folder. An international standard arrived at our design independently, which is good evidence it is correct.\n\nSource: [ATUS activity coding lexicon](https://www.bls.gov/tus/lexicons/lexiconwex2023.pdf)\n\n## 4. What the twelve questions were drawn from\nTwelve kinds of time, cut from the classifications above to what an agent and a calendar can act on. The first three are time that is not negotiable. Four to eight is time committed to something or someone. Nine to eleven is time the client chose. Twelve measures how much of the week the client controls.\n\nSleep. Getting ready. Travel. Paid work. Unpaid work time. Household time. Caring for people. Appointments. Time with people. Rest and leisure. Time on what you are building. Control over your week.\n\nThe questions and their options are held in Folder 01 Time Curriculum.\n\n## 5. What the scale measures\nResolved 28 August 2026. The scale measures how well accounted for a kind of time is. It does not measure how well the client lives. Ten means the client knows where those hours go and it is how they want it. One means they do not know.\n\nA client who sleeps five hours a night on purpose, knows it, and wants it that way is a ten. The scale does not judge the choice.\n\nA low start is a fact about the record, not a verdict on the client. Most clients will start low across most questions, because most people do not account for their time.\n\n## 6. Research behind the teaching layer\nSeneca was a Roman statesman and philosopher who advised the emperor Nero and died in 65 AD. In an essay written around 49 AD, De Brevitate Vitae, meaning On the Shortness of Life, he argued that life is not short but that people waste it. He observed that people guard property fiercely and go to arms over a boundary dispute, while allowing others to take possession of their time without objection.\n\nSource: [Seneca, On the Shortness of Life, full text](https://archive.org/stream/SenecaOnTheShortnessOfLife/Seneca+on+the+Shortness+of+Life_djvu.txt)\n\nDaniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky were psychologists whose work on how people misjudge risk and probability changed economics. Kahneman received the Nobel Prize in Economics in 2002. In 1979 they named the planning fallacy: people underestimate how long a task will take even when they have done it many times before. Three psychologists, Roger Buehler, Dale Griffin and Michael Ross, tested it in 1994 by asking students to predict when they would finish their theses. The average prediction was under thirty four days and most took considerably longer.\n\nSource: [Buehler, Griffin and Ross, Exploring the planning fallacy, 1994](https://web.mit.edu/curhan/www/docs/Articles/biases/67_J_Personality_and_Social_Psychology_366,_1994.pdf)\n\nSophie Leroy is an organizational psychologist at the University of Washington. In 2009 she established what she called attention residue. When a person switches tasks, part of their attention stays on the first one and their performance on the second measurably suffers. Gloria Mark, a professor of informatics at the University of California, Irvine, found that after an interruption a worker takes around twenty five minutes to return to what they were doing. This is the evidence behind treating the longest unbroken block as more useful than the total.\n\nSource: [Sophie Leroy on attention residue, University of Washington](https://www.uwb.edu/news/2024/05/17/an-ongoing-study-on-the-success-of-staying-focused)\n\n## 7. Open decisions\n* Pick the discretionary hours threshold we consider healthy, and say why. The literature offers ten hours a week, two hours a day, or a relative measure against the median.\n\n* Question eight, obligations you did not choose, may belong in Legal rather than Time. It is real, it consumes days, and no classification system counts it well.\n\n## 8. Superseded\n**The single shared numeric scale.** An earlier draft of this entry proposed twelve subjects scored against one shared ruler. Superseded 28 August 2026. The Language Standard, section 9, requires twelve questions each presenting ten written options.\n\n# Entry: October 11, 2026\nFolder 01 Time changed on October 11, 2026. Version 4 measures how a client plans commitments, carries them out, reviews what happened, and follows through. This entry holds the evidence for that definition and a check of each August 28 source.\n\nSource material is the published reviews of time management, planning, and progress monitoring, the instruments occupational therapists use to assess time management, the World Health Organization classification of functioning, and the Bureau of Labor Statistics release that the August 28 entry cited.\n\nEach source named below was read on October 11, 2026, in full or as its published abstract, unless section 9 lists it. A figure from a source in section 9 is held until that source is read.\n\n## 1. The finding that changes the folder\nTime management has been measured across 158 studies and 53,957 people. It is related to wellbeing more strongly than to performance. The link with wellbeing is r \\= .313. The link with job performance is r \\= .259. The link with scores on standardized tests is r \\= .011.\n\nT. H. Macan studied 353 employees in 1994. Time management behaviors were related to lower tension and higher job satisfaction. They were not related to job performance as rated by supervisors.\n\nThis matters because the folder cannot promise that a client will get more done. The evidence supports a smaller claim. A client who plans and reviews carries less strain. That fits Version 4, which does not measure how much a client does.\n\nMost of these studies describe students and employed adults at one point in time. The check found no study of adults without stable housing and no study of adults on shift work. The search for one was not exhaustive.\n\nSource: [Aeon, Faber and Panaccio, Does time management work? A meta-analysis, 2021](https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0245066)\n\nSource: [Macan, Time management: test of a process model, 1994](https://doi.org/10.1037/0021-9010.79.3.381)\n\n## 2. The structure the field uses\nResearchers describe carrying out an intention as a cycle with four parts. A person decides what to commit to, plans it, acts on it, and reviews what happened. The review is used in the next plan.\n\nBrad Aeon and Herman Aguinis define time management as “a form of decision making used by individuals to structure, protect, and adapt their time to changing conditions.”\n\n**Structure.** Planning what will happen and when.\n\n**Protect.** Deciding what to take on and what to decline.\n\n**Adapt.** Changing the plan when conditions change.\n\nThe parts are connected. Time management is related to conscientiousness at r \\= .451, so the parts share one underlying disposition. An occupational therapy instrument with 30 items was tested in 2022. Its items formed three groups: time management, organization and planning, and regulation of emotion. Neither result supports twelve separate abilities.\n\nSource: [Roshanay and others, Occupational Therapy International, 2022](https://doi.org/10.1155/2022/6949102)\n\n## 3. What helps a person carry out a plan\nThree practices have the strongest support.\n\n**A plan that names when, where, and how.** Across 642 tests, a plan in the form “if this happens, I will do that” improved outcomes. The effects ran from d \\= .27 to d \\= .66. The effect was larger when the person wanted the outcome and rehearsed the plan.\n\n**A review that is put on file.** Across 138 experiments with 19,951 people, a prompt to check progress improved results, d \\= 0.40. The effect was larger when the progress was written down or reported to another person. This is the evidence behind Daily Close.\n\n**A reminder.** In a Cochrane review, attendance at health appointments was 67.8 percent with no reminder and 78.6 percent with a text reminder. In a review of 16 trials with 2,742 patients, text messages raised the odds of taking medication as prescribed, with an odds ratio of 2.11. Both reviews cover health care.\n\nA recurring action takes longer than a month to become automatic. In a study of 96 volunteers, the time ran from 18 to 254 days. Missing one day did not change the result.\n\nSource: [Sheeran, Listrom and Gollwitzer, The when and how of planning, 2025](https://doi.org/10.1080/10463283.2024.2334563)\n\nSource: [Harkin and others, Does monitoring goal progress promote goal attainment?, 2016](https://doi.org/10.1037/bul0000025)\n\nSource: [Cochrane review, Mobile phone messaging reminders for attendance at healthcare appointments, 2013](https://doi.org/10.1002/14651858.CD007458.pub3)\n\nSource: [Thakkar and others, Mobile telephone text messaging for medication adherence in chronic disease, 2016](https://doi.org/10.1001/jamainternmed.2015.7667)\n\nSource: [Lally and others, How are habits formed, 2010](https://doi.org/10.1002/ejsp.674)\n\n## 4. Support and what a client does alone\nThe World Health Organization classification of functioning keeps two measures apart.\n\n**Capacity.** What a person does with no help.\n\n**Performance.** What a person does in their real setting, including the devices and the people who help them.\n\nThe classification files both. It does not lower performance because help was used.\n\nThis is the model for Time. What a client does with the schedule and the agent is one fact. What the client does alone is a second fact. The amount of support is a third.\n\nTwo older rating scales take the opposite position. Each lowers a score when a person has help from another person. Treating support as neutral is a program position.\n\nKnowing when to rely on a reminder is itself a skill. In laboratory studies people set more reminders than they needed. Lower confidence in their own memory predicted it.\n\nOne trial tested time management training for adults with attention deficit hyperactivity disorder, autism, or a mental disorder. It randomized 88 people, and 75 gave data at the start. A group program and individual occupational therapy both improved time management skills. Neither format did better than the other on the main outcome.\n\nSource: [World Health Organization, Towards a Common Language for Functioning, Disability and Health](https://cdn.who.int/media/docs/default-source/classification/icf/icfbeginnersguide.pdf)\n\nSource: [Lidström Holmqvist and others, Effectiveness of a group-based time-management intervention, 2026](https://doi.org/10.1080/09638288.2026.2614218)\n\n## 5. What the twelve questions are drawn from\nTwelve abilities were proposed on October 11, 2026. They are not approved.\n\nCommitments. The plan. Estimates. Preparation and travel. Available time. Starting. Completing. Changes. Review. Consequences. Remaining work. Recurring responsibilities.\n\nThe research supports nine of the twelve after rewording. It points to three changes.\n\n**Merge estimates with preparation and travel.** Both measure how long a commitment takes in full.\n\n**Replace available time with taking on a new commitment.** A question about open hours assumes a client controls their week. Protecting time is one of the three parts in section 2, and no question covers it.\n\n**Add use of support.** Section 4 shows it is a skill, and no question covers it.\n\nThe twelve are hypotheses. Related research does not validate them. Validation needs answers from clients.\n\nThe twelve kinds of time in the August 28 entry stay in use. They are the kinds of time on the schedule.\n\nThe questions and their options will be held in Folder 01 Time Curriculum. That document is not revised yet.\n\n## 6. What the scale measures\nProposed October 11, 2026. The scale measures how well a client plans a commitment and carries it out under the conditions the client set. It does not measure how much a client does.\n\nFour findings affect how the options are written.\n\n**An option describes what the client does.** Four of the proposed top options describe the schedule, with wording such as “is on my schedule without effort.” A client with a working agent would select ten whatever the client does alone.\n\n**“Without effort” cannot be observed.** The Language Standard requires a start option that another person could observe.\n\n**“Every” and “each” set one standard for all clients.** Version 4 compares an outcome with the conditions that client set.\n\n**A client cannot report accuracy before it is measured.** How close an estimate is, and how often a plan is carried out, need outcomes on file. A start selected at Enrollment is the client’s own report. The research supports selecting it again after a period of Plans and Daily Close outcomes. No study gives the length of that period.\n\nTen written options have not been tested. Whether a client can tell ten levels apart is unknown.\n\n## 7. Corrections to the August 28 entry\nThe August 28 text stays as written. Each correction below is dated October 11, 2026.\n\n**Leisure hours, section 1.** The figures are correct: 5.2 hours of leisure and sports a day, and 2.6 hours of television. Two limits apply. The 2025 survey has no data for September 30 through November 11, 2025. It interviewed about 6,100 people.\n\n**No group under 4.5 hours, section 1.** The statement is from Sturm and Cohen and uses 2014 to 2016 data from 32,048 people. It describes group averages. It does not describe one person.\n\n**No idea where it goes, section 1.** No source supports this sentence. The check found no direct evidence that free time reaches a client in pieces too short to use.\n\n**Three parts of a day, section 2.** The scheme has four parts: necessary, contracted, committed, and free time.\n\n**Ten hours a week, section 2.** Clair Vickery did not propose ten hours of discretionary time. A 2016 paper reports that her figure was 81.4 hours a week of necessary time, with necessary leisure included. The August 28 sentence is withdrawn.\n\n**Picking a threshold, sections 2 and 7.** No study establishes a healthy number of discretionary hours. A threshold needs a program decision. The evidence does not supply one.\n\n**Seventeen categories and travel, section 3.** Correct. The survey has 17 major categories and codes travel by its purpose. The sentence that calls this good evidence the design is correct is withdrawn. Two classification systems that agree do not show the design fits a different use.\n\n**The planning fallacy, section 6.** The thesis figures are correct. Students predicted 33.9 days and took 55.5. The fallacy is established for long tasks with many steps. Reviews report that people overestimate short tasks. The statement that people underestimate a task they have done many times is too broad.\n\n**Twenty five minutes, section 6.** The figure is misdescribed. Mark, Gonzalez and Harris observed 24 workers. Interrupted work was resumed after 25 minutes 26 seconds on average, and the worker did other work in between. That is the time before returning to the work. It is not the time needed to regain focus. A second figure in wide use, 23 minutes 15 seconds, comes from a 2006 Gallup interview with Gloria Mark.\n\n**The longest unbroken block, section 6.** The check found no study that compares one unbroken block with the same total time in pieces. It stays as a program position.\n\n**The prize, section 6.** The full name is the Sveriges Riksbank Prize in Economic Sciences in Memory of Alfred Nobel.\n\nSource: [US Bureau of Labor Statistics, American Time Use Survey, 2025 Results](https://www.bls.gov/news.release/pdf/atus.pdf)\n\nSource: [Sturm and Cohen, Free time and physical activity among Americans 15 years or older, 2019](https://doi.org/10.5888/pcd16.190017)\n\nSource: [Williams, Masuda and Tallis, A measure whose time has come: formalizing time poverty, 2016](https://doi.org/10.1007/s11205-015-1029-z)\n\nSource: [Mark, Gonzalez and Harris, No task left behind? Examining the nature of fragmented work, 2005](https://doi.org/10.1145/1054972.1055017)\n\n## 8. Research behind the teaching layer\nThree lessons have strong support. They are the three practices in section 3.\n\nThree statements in the Curriculum have no support as written.\n\n**An eight hour shift is a ten or eleven hour commitment.** No source gives this figure. The 2025 survey reports 8.1 hours worked on days worked by full time workers. The lesson holds when the client adds up their own day.\n\n**The gap is almost always larger than they expect.** No source gives this, and people overestimate short tasks.\n\n**Total free hours is close to useless.** A study of 35,375 Americans found that wellbeing changes with the amount of discretionary time. The total carries information.\n\nSix statements are program positions. No study establishes them.\n\n* A changed time is not a failure.\n\n* Rest counts the same as every other commitment.\n\n* Doing an action and finishing the responsibility are two facts.\n\n* The client sets what completes a commitment.\n\n* Support does not lower an assessment.\n\n* Hours a client cannot account for are not counted as free.\n\nSource: [Sharif, Mogilner and Hershfield, Having too little or too much time is linked to lower subjective well-being, 2021](https://doi.org/10.1037/pspp0000391)\n\n## 9. Sources still to be read\nA second check on October 11, 2026 went back to each paper. It ran out of search allowance and did not reach the ten sources below. Each one is cited in this entry from the first search or from a later paper that describes it.\n\n* Heckhausen and Gollwitzer, 1987, on the four parts of carrying out an intention.\n\n* Roshanay and others, 2022, on the three groups of items.\n\n* Harkin and others, 2016, on checking progress.\n\n* The World Health Organization guide to its classification of functioning.\n\n* The Functional Independence Measure, and Lawton and Brody, 1969. These are the two older rating scales in section 4.\n\n* Gilbert and others, 2020, on the use of reminders.\n\n* Ås, 1978, on the four parts of a day.\n\n* Vickery, 1977. Her figure is known here through Williams, Masuda and Tallis.\n\n* Buehler, Griffin and Ross, 1994. The two figures were read in a 2010 review by the same authors.\n\n* Halkjelsvik and Jørgensen, 2012, on short tasks.\n\n## 10. Open decisions\n* Whether each question is answered twice: once for what the client does alone, and once for what the client does with the schedule and the agent.\n\n* Whether the three changes in section 5 are made to the twelve.\n\n* Whether a start selected at Enrollment is selected again after a period of outcomes, and how long that period is.\n\n* Whether a client can tell ten options apart. This needs a test with clients.\n\n* Whether the twelve are twelve abilities, three, or one. This needs answers from clients.\n\n## 11. Superseded\n**The finding that changes the pitch.** August 28, section 1. Superseded October 11, 2026. Version 4 does not promise to show a client the free hours they already have. It promises help with planning a commitment, carrying it out, and reviewing it. The hours a client controls stay as one figure the schedule produces.\n\n**What the twelve questions were drawn from.** August 28, section 4. Superseded October 11, 2026. The twelve kinds of time are no longer the twelve questions. They are the kinds of time on the schedule.\n\n**What the scale measures.** August 28, section 5. Superseded October 11, 2026. The scale no longer measures how well accounted for a kind of time is.\n\n**Both open decisions.** August 28, section 7. Superseded October 11, 2026. The threshold decision is withdrawn in section 7 of this entry. The question about obligations a client did not choose no longer applies, since appointments are no longer one of the twelve questions.\n\n# Entry: October 11, 2026. Verification\nThe first October 11 entry listed ten sources in its section 9 that had not been read. This entry reports the check of each one. It corrects the first October 11 entry where a source says otherwise. Both earlier entries stay as written.\n\nSource material is the ten sources themselves, in the published paper, the publisher’s abstract, or a document from the institution that issued them.\n\nThe twelve proposed questions and their options stay proposals. Sections 5 and 6 of the first October 11 entry hold the recommendations for the Curriculum review.\n\n## 1. The ten sources\n**Harkin and others, 2016.** Read in full, in the authors’ accepted manuscript. The figures are confirmed: 138 studies, 19,951 people, every study randomized, and an effect on reaching the outcome of d \\= 0.40. Section 2 states the limits.\n\n**The World Health Organization guide.** Read in full. The three statements are confirmed on pages 11 and 12. Performance includes the devices and the personal assistance a person uses. Capacity is assessed with no assistance. Both can be coded with and without assistance.\n\n**The Functional Independence Measure.** Read in the 2014 federal assessment form that carries it. It has seven levels. Levels 7 and 6 have no helper. Level 6 applies when a device is used. Levels 5 to 1 have a helper. The statement in the first October 11 entry is confirmed.\n\n**Lawton and Brody, 1969.** The paper was not read. The scale was read in a reproduction from the Hartford Institute for Geriatric Nursing. The statement in the first October 11 entry is corrected in section 2.\n\n**Gilbert and others, 2020.** Experiments 1 and 2 were read in full, and experiment 3 in the abstract. People set more reminders than was best for them. Lower confidence in their own memory was related to it, with a correlation of negative .31 in experiment 2. The authors state that confidence does not explain all of it.\n\n**Roshanay and others, 2022.** Read in full. The sample was 241 adults from the general population. The 30 items did not measure one trait. The wording in the first October 11 entry is corrected in section 2.\n\n**Heckhausen and Gollwitzer, 1987.** Read as an abstract. The paper reports two experiments on a person’s state of mind before and after a decision. The sentence in the first October 11 entry is corrected in section 2.\n\n**Ås, 1978.** Read in a scan held by the Centre for Time Use Research. The four kinds of time are confirmed: necessary, contracted, committed, and free.\n\n**Vickery, 1977.** Not read. The publisher’s copy could not be opened. Two later papers give the same account. Vickery counted 10.2 hours a day of personal maintenance and added ten hours a week of necessary leisure. That makes 81.4 hours a week of necessary time. The correction in the first October 11 entry stands on those two papers.\n\n**Buehler, Griffin and Ross, 1994.** Read in full. There were 37 students. They predicted 33.9 days and took 55.5. Fewer than one third, 29.7 percent, finished by the date they predicted.\n\n**Halkjelsvik and Jørgensen, 2012.** Read as an abstract. The statement in the first October 11 entry is corrected in section 2.\n\nSource: [Harkin and others, Does monitoring goal progress promote goal attainment?, accepted manuscript, 2016](https://eprints.whiterose.ac.uk/id/eprint/87431/)\n\nSource: [World Health Organization, Towards a Common Language for Functioning, Disability and Health, 2002](https://cdn.who.int/media/docs/default-source/classification/icf/icfbeginnersguide.pdf)\n\nSource: [Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services, Inpatient Rehabilitation Facility Patient Assessment Instrument, 2014](https://www.cms.gov/Medicare/Medicare-Fee-for-Service-Payment/InpatientRehabFacPPS/Downloads/IRF-PAI-FINAL-for-Use-Oct2014-updated-v4.pdf)\n\nSource: [Lawton and Brody, Assessment of older people, 1969](https://doi.org/10.1093/geront/9.3_Part_1.179)\n\nSource: [Gilbert and others, Optimal use of reminders, 2020](https://doi.org/10.1037/xge0000652)\n\nSource: [Roshanay and others, The Psychometric Properties of the Original Version of Assessment of Time Management Skills, 2022](https://doi.org/10.1155/2022/6949102)\n\nSource: [Heckhausen and Gollwitzer, Thought contents and cognitive functioning in motivational versus volitional states of mind, 1987](https://doi.org/10.1007/BF00992338)\n\nSource: [Ås, Studies of time-use: problems and prospects, 1978](https://www.timeuse.org/sites/ctur/files/public/ctur_journal_article/2179/DagfinnAs.pdf)\n\nSource: [Antonopoulos and Memis, Levy Economics Institute Working Paper No. 600, 2010](https://www.levyinstitute.org/pubs/wp_600a.pdf)\n\nSource: [Buehler, Griffin and Ross, Exploring the planning fallacy, 1994](https://doi.org/10.1037/0022-3514.67.3.366)\n\nSource: [Halkjelsvik and Jørgensen, From origami to software development, 2012](https://doi.org/10.1037/a0025996)\n\n## 2. Corrections to the first October 11 entry\nEach correction below is dated October 11, 2026.\n\n**Daily Close, section 3.** Harkin and others supports progress monitoring. It does not test Daily Close. No study in it is a daily conversation with another person about the commitments of that day. The authors state that only one study concerned a subject unrelated to health. The effect falls from d \\= 0.40 to d \\= 0.19 after their adjustment for unpublished studies. A prompt to review what a person set out to do added no significant benefit. The sentence that calls this the evidence behind Daily Close is withdrawn. Daily Close is a design that follows from the finding. No study has tested it.\n\n**What the finding does support, section 3.** Two parts of the finding are closest to Daily Close. The effect was larger when progress was reported to another person, d \\= 0.47, than when it was kept private, d \\= 0.19. The effect was larger when progress was written down, d \\= 0.43, than when it was not, d \\= 0.29.\n\n**Capacity and performance, section 4.** The World Health Organization distinction is a framework for thinking about assistance. It is not a validated scoring instrument for Time. The guide gives a way to code what a person does with and without assistance. The World Health Organization built a separate instrument for assessment. No tested scale exists for what a client does alone and what a client does with the schedule and the agent.\n\n**Two older rating scales, section 4.** The statement holds for the Functional Independence Measure. For the Lawton scale it holds in part. Help from another person lowers the score for shopping, medication, meals, and laundry. It does not lower the score for housekeeping, travel, or money.\n\n**Reminders as a skill, section 4.** The task was a memory task of about one minute, on a tablet or a computer. One experiment had 41 adults in a laboratory. One had 268 adults in the United States online. The study did not observe the reminders a person uses in daily life.\n\n**Time management training, section 4.** Both groups in the trial received training. No group went without. The trial shows that both groups improved. It does not show that the training caused the improvement.\n\n**A cycle with four parts, section 2.** The 1987 paper does not set out four parts. A 1990 chapter by Gollwitzer names four phases: deciding, planning, acting, and evaluating. The idea that reflection on one attempt is used in the next plan comes from Zimmerman, 2000, who names three phases: forethought, performance, and self reflection. Neither was read. Both are known through later papers.\n\n**Three groups of items, section 2.** The three groups were taken from an earlier Swedish study and then tested on this sample. They held for 27 of the 30 items.\n\n**The planning fallacy, section 7.** Two sentences are withdrawn: that the fallacy is established for long tasks with many steps, and that reviews report people overestimate short tasks. Halkjelsvik and Jørgensen found underestimation more often in engineering and management studies. They did not find it in psychology studies. They question the finding that small tasks are overestimated and state that random error can produce it in part.\n\n**Short tasks, section 8.** One review, by Roy, Christenfeld and McKenzie in 2005, reports that tasks under five minutes are more likely to be overestimated. It was read as an excerpt of the authors’ copy. The direction of the error is unsettled. The lesson holds when a client compares their own estimate with what happened.\n\nSource: [World Health Organization, WHO Disability Assessment Schedule](https://www.who.int/standards/classifications/international-classification-of-functioning-disability-and-health/who-disability-assessment-schedule)\n\nSource: [Roy, Christenfeld and McKenzie, Underestimating the duration of future events, 2005](https://doi.org/10.1037/0033-2909.131.5.738)\n\n## 3. Association and cause\nThe review of 158 studies in section 1 of the first October 11 entry reports correlations. Most of those studies measured people at one point in time. A correlation shows that two measures move together. It does not show that one causes the other.\n\nOne sentence in that section is corrected. It said a client who plans and reviews carries less strain. The evidence supports this much: people who report more time management also report higher wellbeing. The studies do not show which one comes first.\n\nCause is shown in three places. The 138 studies of progress monitoring were randomized. The appointment reminder studies were randomized trials. The medication reminder studies were randomized trials.\n\n## 4. What supports the practice and what is analogy\nEach finding gives Time one of four kinds of support.\n\n**Supports the practice.** A study tested the same practice in another setting.\n\n**Analogy.** A study tested a different task or a different group of people. The finding is applied to Time by inference.\n\n**Framework.** A way to organize the subject. No test stands behind its use in Time.\n\n**Association.** Two measures move together. No cause is shown.\n\n| Finding | What was studied | Support for Time |\n| :---- | :---- | :---- |\n| A text reminder before an appointment | Randomized trials in health care | Supports the practice |\n| A plan that names when, where, and how | 642 tests across many settings | Supports the practice |\n| Progress written down and reported to another person | 138 randomized studies, all but one on health | Supports the practice |\n| Daily Close | No study | Design inference from the row above |\n| Scoring what a client does alone apart from what a client does with support | A World Health Organization coding framework | Framework |\n| Knowing when to rely on a reminder | A memory task of about one minute | Analogy |\n| The time for a recurring action to become automatic | 96 volunteers, one daily action each, 12 weeks | Analogy |\n| Completion dates predicted too early | 37 students and their theses | Analogy |\n| The parts of carrying out an intention | Theory | Framework |\n| Time management and wellbeing | 158 studies, most at one point in time | Association |\n| Training in time management | One trial, two kinds of training, no group without training | Both groups improved. No cause shown |\n\nNo finding in this table was tested inside Universe City.\n\n## 5. Still unverified\n* Vickery, 1977. Her figures are known through two later papers.\n\n* Zimmerman, 2000, and Gollwitzer, 1990. Each is known through later papers.\n\n* Lawton and Brody, 1969. The scale was read in a reproduction.\n\n* Halkjelsvik and Jørgensen, 2012, and Heckhausen and Gollwitzer, 1987. Each was read as an abstract.\n\n* Roy, Christenfeld and McKenzie, 2005. The sentence on tasks under five minutes was read as an excerpt.\n\n* The table in Harkin and others that lists how often each study asked people to monitor, and for how long.\n\n* The setting of experiment 2 in Gilbert and others. The paper does not state it.\n\n## 6. Open decisions\nThese are decided before the Curriculum is written.\n\n* Whether Daily Close is described as a program design that follows from research on progress monitoring. The evidence does not support describing it as tested.\n\n* Whether each question is answered twice: once for what the client does alone, and once for what the client does with the schedule and the agent. No tested scale exists for this.\n\n* Whether the three changes in section 5 of the first October 11 entry are made to the twelve.\n\n* Whether a start selected at Enrollment is selected again after a period of outcomes, and how long that period is.\n\n* Whether ten options are tested with clients before the Curriculum is final.\n\n* Whether the lesson on estimates drops its claim about the direction of the error.\n\n* Whether text a client reads states that planning improves wellbeing. The evidence shows an association.\n\n## 7. Superseded\n**Sources still to be read.** First October 11 entry, section 9. Superseded October 11, 2026 by sections 1 and 5 of this entry.\n\n**The evidence behind Daily Close.** First October 11 entry, section 3. Superseded October 11, 2026. The study supports progress monitoring. Daily Close is a design inference.\n\n**A cycle with four parts.** First October 11 entry, section 2. Superseded October 11, 2026 by the correction in section 2 of this entry.\n\n**Short tasks.** First October 11 entry, sections 7 and 8. Superseded October 11, 2026. The direction of the error is unsettled.\n\nGoverned by the Document Standard for formatting. Governed by the Language Standard for wording.\n"}];
